# Component pins — the single source of truth for what Vessel builds.
#
# Every build script sources this file, clones/fetches the repo into
# native/<name>, checks out the exact ref, applies patches/<name>/*.patch,
# and builds. Bumping a version here is the entire update procedure:
# change the ref, push, CI rebuilds that component and publishes a new .wcp.
#
# CI watches this file: a change to <NAME>_REF or patches/<name>/ triggers
# exactly the matching workflow, nothing else.
#
# shellcheck disable=SC2034

# --- Translation engines -----------------------------------------------------

# Box64 — x86_64 interpreter/JIT. Ships the SD8EG5 preset that matches Oryon.
BOX64_REPO=https://github.com/ptitSeb/box64.git
BOX64_REF=v0.4.4

# FEX-Emu — primary engine. Built as Windows PE DLLs (arm64ec + wow64),
# loaded inside Wine. Tagged monthly.
FEX_REPO=https://github.com/FEX-Emu/FEX.git
FEX_REF=FEX-2608
# Vessel's own patch revision on top of that tag. The component store is keyed
# by type and version code, so a rebuild that carries a new patch but the same
# upstream tag installs nothing and looks exactly like a patch that did not
# work. Bump this whenever patches/fex/ changes what the build produces.
#   0 -> 260800, 1 -> 260801, and the next tag FEX-2609 is 260900, still above.
# 1: patches/fex/0003 (log level ceiling) and 0004 (the ARM64EC notify deadlock).
# 2: 0004 again, without thread_local -- v1 faulted inside FEX during startup
#    because these hooks run before TLS is usable for a BT-loaded module.
# 3: patches/fex/0005. A per-app JSON config that will not parse killed the
#    process from ProcessInit, two lines before Logging::Init runs -- so it
#    presented as an unexplained c000001d at libarm64ecfex.dll +0x1747E4 and
#    took every tools/gfx harness with it.
# 4: patches/fex/0007. FEXOfflineCompiler could not be made to say anything.
#    Logging::Init reads SilentLog through the config, but every caller reaches
#    it before a config layer is loaded, so it only ever saw the compiled-in
#    default of true, returned without installing a handler, and was never
#    called again -- FEX_SILENTLOG=0 was inert on all three entry points, and
#    the tool's 180 s hang produced two fmt::print lines and no LogMan output
#    of any kind. The same patch stops GenerateCache handing LoadConfig an
#    empty envp, which had made every FEX_* variable inert for cache
#    generation. Bumping is not optional here: the store keys on the version
#    code, so at 260803 the phone would keep the silent build and the next run
#    would look exactly like a fix that did not work.
# 5: patches/fex/0008. With 0007's logging on, the device named the hang:
#    fexofflinecompiler64.exe on amd_fidelityfx_loader_dx12.dll produced
#    155,039 log lines that were all the same line, seh:RtlInitializeExtended
#    Context2 at the identical context address, against exactly one
#    dispatch_exception -- one fault prepared, "repaired", resumed and
#    re-faulted forever. OvercommitTracker::HandleAccessViolation answered
#    "continue execution" on the interval query alone, discarding
#    NtQueryVirtualMemory's status and VirtualAlloc's return. The 32-bit
#    compiler parsed 33 modules and populated 34 caches in the same session,
#    because a plain ARM64 PE loads no EC emulator and its faults take a
#    different path entirely.
# 6: patches/fex/0009. mov to the FS/GS selector in 64-bit mode set
#    DecodeFailure, and a decode failure is a block that can never execute --
#    Requiem hits four GS and one FS of them right after its Denuvo blob
#    loads. Records the selector and deliberately leaves the segment base
#    alone, which is what the hardware does and what Windows needs.
# 7: patches/fex/0010. 0008's diagnostic named the real loop on the next run:
#    13,864,960 consecutive resumes of one page, with NtProtectVirtualMemory
#    returning C000002D. That is STATUS_NOT_COMMITTED, not CONFLICTING_
#    ADDRESSES -- so the page was reserved and empty, not straddling anything.
#    HandleMemoryProtectionNotification classified on Prot alone, so a
#    MEM_RESERVE of PAGE_READWRITE was recorded as guest RWX code; with
#    DisableDEP on that was the offline compiler's own 272 MB LookupCache
#    reservation, whose faults the overcommit handler is supposed to receive.
#    Type now comes through from the notify hooks, matching the two sweeps in
#    the same file that already tested MEM_COMMIT, and a failed reprotect
#    declines instead of reporting a repair that did not happen.
# 8: patches/fex/0011. 0010's MEM_COMMIT gate was wrong and is taken back out.
#    Same game, one revision apart, nothing else changed: on 260806 Metro 2033
#    runs with zero "running the stack out" lines; on 260807 its game process
#    dies -- 27 of them, then virtual_setup_exception stack overflow 3616
#    bytes -- and there are zero RWX reprotect failures anywhere, which is the
#    tell that the handler stopped claiming faults it used to repair. A
#    reservation classified as guest code is still wrong, but a real guest
#    depends on that tracking, and reserved-vs-committed is not the distinction
#    that separates FEX's own memory from the guest's. 0010's failed-reprotect
#    decline is kept: it is what turns the compiler's 13,864,960-iteration spin
#    into a locatable fault, and it did not cause this.
# 9: patches/fex/0010 and 0011 are both gone, and the reason is a measurement
#    that indicts neither of them. 0010's MEM_COMMIT gate was blamed for Metro
#    2033 dying, 0011 took the gate back, and Metro died exactly the same way on
#    the reverted build -- same fault, same modules, msvcr110.dll writing into
#    PhysX3_x64.dll. FEX moved 260806 -> 260807 -> 260808 across three runs and
#    changed nothing; what was constant through every failure was Wine revision
#    13. The pair went in on one install with a Wine bump beside it and was
#    attributed on that, which is not evidence. They come out because a patch
#    whose effect has never been isolated is not one to keep, and the
#    misclassification 0010 aimed at is real but wants the exclusion written up
#    under 0011 in patches/fex/README.md instead.
# 10: 0010 and 0011 both gone; 0001-0009 stay. 0010 came in two halves and both
#     are now measured. The classification half was blamed for Metro 2033 dying,
#     0011 took it back, and Metro died the same way on the reverted build -- so
#     that half was innocent and the real cause of THAT failure was
#     patches/wine/0041, removed and confirmed by its own A/B. What 0011 kept was
#     the decline half, and this is what it costs: with Wine held patch-identical
#     across both runs, Metro on 260806 reached Vulkan device creation and
#     enumerated extensions, while on 260808 it stops after its loader finishes
#     and the X server connects, before any Vulkan call, at 0.2% CPU and no GPU.
#     Declining a fault the guest cannot handle is not better than resuming one it
#     could: the compiler's 13.8M-resume loop is real and still wants fixing, but
#     not with a change that reaches every guest. See patches/fex/README.md for
#     the exclusion that would target the compiler's own reservation instead.
# 11: patches/fex/0010. push and pop of a segment register touched the segment
#     BASE in 64-bit mode, and on Windows gs_cached is the TEB pointer itself
#     (ARM64EC/Module.cpp: `State.gs_cached = TEB`). POPSegmentOp called
#     UpdatePrefixFromSegment unguarded, which walks the GDT -- meaningless in
#     64-bit, where the base comes from the FS/GS MSRs -- and stored a 32-bit
#     descriptor base over the low half of that pointer, so one `pop gs` left
#     every later gs:[...] access reading from a corrupted base. PUSHSegmentOp
#     was the mirror image, pushing <seg>_cached where the hardware pushes a
#     16-bit selector. Together the ordinary push/pop save-restore idiom was
#     destructive rather than merely wrong. MOVSegOp already guards the same
#     call, which is what makes the omission visible once you look. Upstream's
#     own FEX-2302 notes say of segment push/pop that "no one really uses that
#     feature on 64-bit CPUs today" -- anti-tamper code does, which is why
#     Requiem reached it at all, and only after 0009 stopped those blocks
#     failing to decode.
# 12: patches/fex/0011. Cache *loading* killed Requiem before the guest ran an
#     instruction -- two modules loaded, then c0000005 at libarm64ecfex
#     +0x9bb04; moving cache/ aside made the same launch reach Vulkan with 67
#     modules. That RVA disassembles to the movz/movk emit loop inside
#     Arm64Emitter::LoadConstant (`ldr x12, [x0, #8]` / `str w10, [x12]`), a
#     write through the emitter cursor, reached from ApplyCodeRelocations --
#     whose only bounds checks were LOGMAN_THROW_A_FMT and so compiled out of
#     this Release build. LoadCache now validates the whole file structurally
#     before anything is mutated, and refuses with a line naming the invariant;
#     ApplyCodeRelocations refuses to write outside its buffer. A cache that
#     does not validate is skipped and the title runs cold, which is what the
#     nullptr return already meant. Generation, which is where the 13.8M-resume
#     loop lived, is untouched.
# 13: 0010 and 0011 moved aside for one A/B, not deleted. Cache generation
#     drained 52 modules on 260810 with ZERO `RWX reprotect FAILED` lines; on
#     260812, with Wine revision 15 beside it, the loop is back -- 408 and
#     climbing on winmm.dll's two blocks, 54 s of CPU with system time at twice
#     user time, which is what a fault storm looks like from outside. Three
#     things changed between those builds (fex/0010, fex/0011, wine/0041) and
#     they went in together, which is the mistake this file already records
#     twice. FEX is the 15-minute build so it goes first: 0001-0009 is exactly
#     the 260810 set, and if the loop stays away the remaining suspect is
#     wine/0041 -- whose RtlIsEcCode answers feed what FEX believes is code, and
#     therefore which ranges the RWX handler claims.
# 14: patches/fex/0012, and 0010/0011 back on the device -- the A/B cleared all
#     three, so the variable was never the build. It is the module: winmm.dll
#     loops, and the 260810 run that looked clean had skipped it ("Cache up to
#     date"). Two independent sightings, since SessionRuntime.kt's own comment
#     names winmm.dll too. Two blocks, so the 128 MB code buffer is nowhere
#     near exhausted and the relocation-table theory does not apply here.
#     What does: the tool's own 272 MB LookupCache reservation is classified as
#     guest RWX code by DEP promotion, so the SMC handler claims a fault on an
#     uncommitted page, cannot reprotect it (C000002D), reports it handled and
#     resumes -- 5,668,864 times. 0012 excludes ranges the OvercommitTracker
#     owns, which no guest allocation ever reaches, and restores the
#     failed-reprotect decline that was lost when the old 0011 was withdrawn.
#     The decline is the half that fixes the offline compiler, where the
#     tracker and the reservation live in different modules.
# 15: patches/fex/0013, diagnostic only -- no behaviour change on any path that
#     is not already stuck. Requiem now initialises fully and then deadlocks
#     with all 78 threads sleeping; patches/wine/0030 names both critical
#     sections and both holders, and both are the game's own code. The edge
#     from the heap to FEX's code mutex is confirmed by reading (RtlAllocateHeap
#     holds the heap section across NtAllocateVirtualMemory, and with 0002's
#     DisableDEP the allocation notification takes CodeInvalidationMutex
#     exclusively). The reverse edge is not: NtReadFile holds it across a
#     syscall served by Wine's unix side, which uses libc malloc rather than the
#     Win32 heap, and FEXCore allocates from rpmalloc. So the cycle cannot be
#     closed by reading. 0030 cannot close it either -- it only sees
#     RTL_CRITICAL_SECTIONs, and this is a WritePriorityMutex over
#     RtlWaitOnAddress. 0013 records the acquiring site and thread on exclusive
#     acquisition and names the holder when a wait passes five seconds. No fix
#     is shipped against an unproven cycle.
# 16: patches/fex/0013 extended to shared waits. 260815 ran on the device and
#     0013 never fired -- but neither did wine/0030, and 26 of 78 threads sat
#     in futex_wait unattributed, so silence only established that nobody
#     waited *exclusively*. That is not the same as ruling the mutex out.
#     lock_shared() now carries the same instrument: same five-second first
#     wait, same one-shot latch, same per-mutex budget, and the timed reader
#     wait mirrors FutexWaitForReadAvailable exactly -- &Futex+2, 16-bit,
#     Expected >> 16 -- because that pairing with WakeByAddressAll is the part
#     that was audited clean in 2608 and must not drift. Readers record no
#     owner; a stuck reader is by definition blocked by an exclusive holder,
#     which OwnerTID/OwnerSite already name. With both paths covered, silence
#     means no thread waited on this mutex at all, which is a negative worth
#     writing down.
#
#     Also patches/fex/0014, in the same revision because both ship together.
#     0013 fired and named the whole cycle: 0188 holds the Win32 heap section
#     and waits for CodeInvalidationMutex (the DisableDEP notification path);
#     0284 holds CodeInvalidationMutex, taken as ImageTracker::HandleImageMap's
#     first statement, and waits for the heap section. The second edge is ours
#     to fix cheaply: that function held the mutex across std::filesystem, the
#     CodeMapWriter's file open and LoadAOTImages' ScopedUnicodeString, all of
#     which allocate from the *Win32 process heap* rather than rpmalloc, while
#     only three publications actually need the lock. 0014 prepares outside and
#     publishes under it. Edge A is left alone deliberately -- it is created by
#     0002's DisableDEP, and the alternative fix lives in the function that
#     already cost one working game.
# 17: patches/fex/0015. A code map records an image under whatever
#     NtQuerySection gives for its backing file, and a manually mapped section
#     has none -- so generation spawned a child that called CreateFileA(""), and
#     the run ended with "Couldn't find image:" naming nothing and "Cache
#     generation failed for <id>" naming a hash. One nameless module read as the
#     whole cache being broken. Skipped in the parent now, where it costs no
#     process and the other dependencies still generate.
FEX_REVISION=22

# --- Wine --------------------------------------------------------------------

# Upstream Wine, not a fork. ARM64EC is mainline now, which is the whole reason
# this moved: bylaws/wine was the place FEX/ARM64EC integration lived ahead of
# mainline, but its newest branch (upstream-arm64ec) still builds 10.13 from
# August 2025 and nothing there tracks 11.x.
#
# What mainline gives us, checked in the 11.14 tree rather than assumed:
#   - configure knows --enable-archs=arm64ec
#   - ntdll has the real ARM64EC machinery: is_arm64ec(), hybrid image loading,
#     redirect_arm64ec_rva(), a separate arm64ec unix call dispatcher
#   - load_arm64ec_module() reads
#     HKLM\Software\Microsoft\Wow64\amd64 and loads C:\windows\system32\<value>,
#     defaulting to xtajit64.dll. Pointing that value at libarm64ecfex.dll is
#     how FEX plugs in, so the emulator hook is a registry entry, not a patch.
#
# Dropping the fork also dropped a patch: 0001 existed only to fix a shm_open
# call in bylaws' FEX stats hook, and that code is not upstream.
WINE_REPO=https://github.com/ValveSoftware/wine.git
# Valve's experimental branch, not proton_11.0, and the difference is 461
# commits on the same Wine 11.0 base. Four of them land on problems measured
# here: ARM64EC cooperative suspend, "don't hold the InSyscallCallback flag
# during the NtReadFile syscall" (the Wine-side twin of patches/fex/0004),
# "winevulkan: do not show debug assertion dialog on Unix side exceptions"
# (which is the _wassert vkCreateSwapchainKHR that killed Requiem), and
# D3D12_FENCE_SUBMIT_INFO support in win32u_vkQueueSubmit. All 25 patches in
# patches/wine/ were checked against it and apply unchanged.
#
# Note this is Wine *11.0*: there is no Proton on a newer Wine base. Valve
# rebases the Proton delta onto a newer stable only occasionally and has not
# done so for 11.x, so choosing Proton means trading 14 Wine point releases
# for ~1,660 Valve patches.
WINE_REF=experimental_11.0
# A branch moves. Frozen to the commit that was tested, for the reason the
# Mesa pin below records: a branch advancing mid-session silently changed what
# was built. Bump deliberately, never by accident.
WINE_EXACT=19d8528d64db0090f59779386b962e6a9ca40c38
# Vessel's patch revision on top of that tag -- see FEX_REVISION for why this
# exists at all. 1: patches/wine/0018 (null swapchain surface) and 0019 (a log
# line longer than 1019 bytes killing the process).
# 2: patches/wine/0027, the swapchain extent clamped to maxImageExtent.
# 3: patches/wine/0028, the whole swapchain create_info and its pNext chain.
# 4: patches/wine/0029, the futexwho channel. Requiem reaches a created
#    swapchain and then never draws, with 492,808 RtlWakeAddressAll against 259
#    RtlWaitOnAddress on one address -- a wake storm that starves the wait,
#    because RtlWaitOnAddress returns without queueing when the compared value
#    has changed. The counts came from `sync`; this names the thread.
# 5: no patch — the build gained GnuTLS. Wine's bcrypt had no unix backend
#    at all (dlls/bcrypt/gnutls.c is wrapped in HAVE_GNUTLS_CIPHER_INIT and
#    the sysroot shipped no GnuTLS), so every asymmetric call returned
#    STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED. The payload changes, so the code must too.
# 6: no patch — gmp is now built shared. Revision 5 shipped and ran, and every
#    session logged
#      winediag:gnutls_process_attach Compiled without DH support.
#    because bcrypt gates DH on `HAVE_GMP_H && SONAME_LIBGMP` and only the
#    first was defined: WINE_CHECK_SONAME link-tested -lgmp successfully
#    against a static libgmp.a and then found no NEEDED entry to name. The
#    payload gains libgmp.so and bcrypt.so gains the DH paths, so the code
#    must move again — same reason as 5.
# 7: no patch — the build gained a Media Foundation backend. Revisions up to 6
#    shipped a complete-looking PE Media Foundation (mfplat, mf, mfreadwrite,
#    mfmediaengine, mfsrcsnk, mfmp4srcsnk, mfasfsrcsnk all present in
#    lib/wine/aarch64-windows) on top of NOTHING: lib/wine/aarch64-unix had no
#    winegstreamer.so, and winedmo.so was there but compiled without FFmpeg.
#    Every media open therefore ended at mfsrcsnk/media_source.c's fallback,
#      Failed to create SourceReader with registered byte stream handler.
#      error(c00d36c4)     MF_E_UNSUPPORTED_BYTESTREAM_TYPE
#      ole:com_get_class_object class {317df618-...} not registered
#    which is Resident Evil Requiem's modal dialog. The payload gains
#    winegstreamer.so, a real winedmo.so, GLib, the GStreamer libraries and
#    plugins, and FFmpeg — so the code must move, same reason as 5 and 6.
# 8: patches/wine/0033. wine.inf's [InfFiles] gives every .inf a display name
#    as a string resource *inside the driver that owns it* --
#    wineusb.inf,"@%12%\wineusb.sys,-1" -- and build/wine.sh configures
#    --without-usb, so wineusb.sys is never built. setupapi then reads a
#    resource out of a file that does not exist and logs
#      copy error 1812   (ERROR_RESOURCE_DATA_NOT_FOUND)
#    four times on every provisioning pass. Confirmed against the revision 7
#    package: 38 .sys drivers present, no wineusb anywhere, and exactly one
#    wineusb line in share/wine/wine.inf. Revision 7 is installed on the
#    device, so a payload that kept its code would install as bytes the store
#    already has -- see FEX_REVISION for that failure mode.
# 9: patches/wine/0035. dnsapi had no unix backend at all on Android --
#    configure's HAVE_RESOLV probe opens with `_res.options & RES_INIT` and
#    bionic has no `_res`, so the test fails to COMPILE, libresolv.c (which is
#    `#ifdef HAVE_RESOLV` end to end) produced an empty object, and the shipped
#    dnsapi.so was a 4,992-byte stub exporting neither __wine_unix_call_funcs
#    nor __wine_unix_lib_init. Every session logged
#      dnsapi:DllMain No libresolv support, expect problems
#    four times and every DnsQuery_W failed. The new backend drives
#    android_res_nquery/android_res_nresult out of libandroid.so instead.
#    The payload changes, so the code must -- same reason as 5, 6 and 7.
# 10: patches/wine/0037 (ole32 DllCanUnloadNow) and 0038 (widl SizeOfImage),
#     both compiled for the first time here. 0038 is a BUILD-TOOL change and
#     forced a second fix: build/wine.sh keyed its cached host-tools tree on
#     SOURCE_SHA alone, which patches never move, so a patched widl would have
#     been skipped and every .winmd kept its old bytes with the build exiting 0.
#     The tools stamp now carries a hash of patches/wine/ as well.
#     Revision 9 was built and packaged in this session, so it is spent even
#     though it never reached the device -- two payloads may not share a code.
# 11: patches/wine/0030, rewritten, because revision 10's instrument could not
#     name an x86-64 caller and said so in the log:
#       sync:RtlpWaitForCriticalSection section 00000000388A7E28 "?" wait timed
#       out in thread 0184, blocked by 021c which took it at 0000006FFFF246B0
#     0000006FFFF246B0 is ntdll+0xe46b0 and its nearest symbol is
#     $ientry_thunk$cdecl$i8$i8 -- ntdll's own ARM64EC entry-thunk table. The
#     lock had been taken by emulated x86-64 code calling in through a thunk, so
#     the recorded return address was the thunk's, and EVERY x64-origin
#     acquisition resolves to that same anonymous region no matter how often it
#     fires. 0030 now also records the first return address in the x29 frame
#     chain that is outside ntdll, which for that path is the guest caller in a
#     module loaddll has named; and it records in RtlTryEnterCriticalSection as
#     well, which is how a heap-owned section printed `which took it at 0`
#     (docs/TODO.md #49). Revision 10 is installed on the device, so a payload
#     that kept its code would install as bytes the store already has -- see
#     FEX_REVISION for that failure mode.
# 12: patches/wine/0040. Requiem dies immediately after its Denuvo blob loads
#     with `virtual_setup_exception stack overflow 1776 bytes addr 0x7ffff09bf0
#     stack 0x20910 (0x20000-0x21000-0x420000)` -- byte-identical across runs and
#     across revisions 8 and 11, so deterministic rather than a race. That
#     address is `prepare_exception_arm64ec` itself, and
#     KiUserExceptionDispatcher opens with `sub sp, sp, #0xcd0`, so every
#     delivery costs 3280 bytes and a 4 MB stack is gone after ~1280 of them
#     that do not unwind. The overflow line records the moment the stack ran out
#     and says nothing about what consumed it; 0040 logs the exception code and
#     address for the last ~74 deliveries before death, which is what separates
#     "one delivery re-raising itself" from "guest code recursing". Revision 11
#     is on the device, so the code must move for the payload to reach it.
# 13: patches/wine/0041, and 0040 gains the fault address. Revision 12's
#     instrument answered the question it was built for on the first run: 34
#     deliveries, every one `code c0000005 flags 0 at 0000007FFFF09F44`, the
#     address identical each time, 7200 bytes of guest stack per delivery until
#     4 MB was gone. That address is `RtlIsEcCode +0x10` -- the bitmap load --
#     and the function indexes `Peb->EcCodeBitMap` by `ptr / page_size` with no
#     bound, while `alloc_arm64ec_map` sizes the map for the address space below
#     `address_space_limit` and no further. FEX asks this on every guest branch
#     target under ARM64EC, so one target above the limit reads off the end of
#     the allocation and raises an access violation inside a query whose honest
#     answer is FALSE. 0041 bounds the read. Whoever resumes that fault
#     unrepaired is a separate defect and is still open; this removes the fault
#     the loop is built on.
# 14: patches/wine/0041 removed. It bounded RtlIsEcCode's read of
#     Peb->EcCodeBitMap, which is a real defect -- the function indexes the map
#     by ptr / page_size with no check while alloc_arm64ec_map sizes it for the
#     address space and no further -- but the fix went to the device in the same
#     install as a FEX bump, Metro 2033 stopped working, and the FEX half was
#     blamed and reverted twice with no effect. Across four runs Wine revision 13
#     is the only thing every failure has in common. The likely mechanism is the
#     new guard itself: it caches the map's extent from NtQueryVirtualMemory and
#     returns FALSE if that query does not answer exactly as expected, so a
#     wrong bound makes real EC code report as not-EC, which is a far larger
#     lie than the out-of-range read it was meant to prevent. 0040 stays: the
#     instrument is what found the fault in the first place and has cost
#     nothing. The bound comes back when it is derived from something that
#     cannot be wrong -- address_space_limit, which is what sized the map --
#     rather than from a query at first use.
# 15: patches/wine/0041 back, with the bound derived from HighestUserAddress
#     instead of from NtQueryVirtualMemory at first use. Revision 13's version
#     cached the map's extent from a query that answers with one region rather
#     than the whole reservation, so the bound came out short, real EC addresses
#     began reporting as not-EC, and Metro 2033 went from working to dying in an
#     unrepairable write fault -- msvcr110.dll writing into PhysX3_x64.dll's
#     image, resumed until a 4 MB guest stack was gone. Removing the patch fixed
#     Metro and cost Requiem the fix it needed, which is how both directions got
#     measured. HighestUserAddress cannot be short in that way: it is the same
#     quantity that sized the map, a property of the address space rather than
#     of one allocation's layout, and an address above it is not user code by
#     definition, so the conservative and the correct answer are the same one.
# 17: patches/wine/0030 again, because "took it at 0" was the instrument and
#     not the lock. The site table is one slot per section hashed from its
#     address, and get_site returns NULL when the key no longer matches -- so
#     any other section landing on that slot silently unnames a lock that is
#     still held, and a lock held for two minutes on a busy title is almost
#     certain to be evicted before it is reported. A Requiem session showed both
#     outcomes in the same minute: one stuck section named its holder, the other
#     printed zero. Now 1024 slots, four-way probing, and the slot released when
#     the section is, so a colliding section finds space instead of overwriting.
# 27: patches/wine/0047's report lines moved from TRACE/WARN to MESSAGE. The
#     patch shipped at 26 and the dump was invisible: Vessel runs with
#     WINEDEBUG=-all,err+all,..., so neither the "audio dump: <path>, <rate> Hz"
#     header nor the "cannot open" failure ever reached the log, and a capture
#     that silently did not start is indistinguishable from one that did.
#     Revision 26 is on the device, so the code must move for the payload to
#     reach it -- see FEX_REVISION for that failure mode.
# 31: patches/wine/0052, and 32, and its removal at 33 -- one attempt in three
#     parts, kept as one entry because the lesson is the whole arc and not any
#     one revision of it.
#
#     conhost stored both console mode words verbatim, so
#     SetConsoleMode(ENABLE_VIRTUAL_TERMINAL_PROCESSING) always succeeded and
#     GetConsoleMode always read the bit back, on a console with no VT parser at
#     all: that flag has one use in conhost.c, a wrap-at-EOL corner in
#     write_console, and ENABLE_VIRTUAL_TERMINAL_INPUT appears nowhere in
#     conhost or kernelbase/console.c. WineHQ bug 49780 is the same finding.
#     Python 3.14's REPL, Node's readline and PowerShell 7 all printed their
#     escape sequences as text on the device.
#
#     31 masked the bits out of the stored mode and returned STATUS_SUCCESS.
#     Insufficient, and measurably so: SetConsoleMode still succeeded, so a
#     caller that sets the bit and proceeds rather than reading it back was
#     unaffected. 32 made the call fail with STATUS_INVALID_PARAMETER, on the
#     theory that these programs carry the non-VT fallback pre-VT Windows
#     required. **Also measured, also wrong**: the garbage persisted. Modern
#     cross-platform tools emit VT whatever the console reports, so there is no
#     fallback for a refusal to trigger.
#
#     Removed at 33. What is left of it is the knowledge that no console-mode
#     answer fixes this -- the console has to parse the sequences, which is what
#     upstream draft MR wine!9973 ("conhost: add basic VT control sequence",
#     January 2026) does for SGR and CUP. A patch that changes behaviour and
#     fixes nothing is not one to carry, and the refusal half additionally risks
#     breaking any program that treats a failed SetConsoleMode as fatal.
#
#     The number still moves forward: 32 is installed on the device, and the
#     store keys on the version code, so a Wine built without the patch at code
#     100110032 would install as a no-op and look exactly like a revert that did
#     not take.
# 34: patches/wine/0052, third attempt and the first that addresses the actual
#     problem. 31 and 32 both tried to answer through the console *mode* -- mask
#     the VT bit, then fail SetConsoleMode outright -- and both were measured
#     doing nothing, because Python's REPL, Node's readline and PSReadLine emit
#     VT whatever the console reports. There is no mode answer. conhost has to
#     parse, which is what upstream draft MR wine!9973 concluded too.
#
#     write_console now runs a state machine before the control-character
#     handling: SGR mapped to console attributes, CUP/CUU/CUD/CUF/CUB/CHA, ED
#     and EL in all three variants each, save/restore cursor, DECTCEM for the
#     cursor, and OSC consumed to BEL or ST. The parser state lives on the
#     screen buffer because a sequence can be split across two write_console
#     calls, which readline does routinely.
#
#     The rule it is built on, and the reason it works even where it is
#     incomplete: **an escape sequence must never reach the screen, including
#     one this does not implement.** Every unhandled dispatch swallows. Missing
#     an effect costs a few characters; printing the sequence costs the screen.
#     Alternate screen contents, scroll regions and 256/truecolour are consumed
#     and not rendered, deliberately.
#
#     Gated on ENABLE_VIRTUAL_TERMINAL_PROCESSING as Windows gates it, and
#     skipped when console->tty_output is set -- that path forwards to a real
#     terminal which parses these itself, and consuming them here would eat the
#     effect.
#
#     **Both directions, in one patch.** ENABLE_VIRTUAL_TERMINAL_INPUT was
#     unimplemented too -- the flag appears nowhere in conhost or in
#     kernelbase/console.c -- and its absence is quieter than the output side
#     but just as final: in raw mode a key event carrying no UnicodeChar is
#     dropped, so arrows, Home/End, PgUp/PgDn, Insert/Delete and the function
#     keys reach the program as nothing at all. Rendering the output correctly
#     and then swallowing the arrow keys would have been a strange place to
#     stop. xterm encoding, because PSReadLine, GNU readline and PyREPL all set
#     the flag and then parse xterm: CSI 1 ; m <final> with
#     m = 1 + shift + 2*alt + 4*ctrl, which is what makes Ctrl+Left move a word.
#     Raw mode only -- in line-input mode conhost runs its own editor and a key
#     already has a meaning there.
#
#     Unverified on the device; it compiles and round-trips, and that is all
#     that can be said until a session runs.
# 35: 0052's SGR corrected. Revision 34 ran on the device and the parser works
#     -- Python's REPL came up with a clean coloured prompt and Node's banner
#     with no escape text anywhere, which is what 31 and 32 never achieved. One
#     bug in it: SGR 7 (reverse video) was applied by swapping the foreground
#     and background halves of `attr` in place, and SGR 27 (reverse off) was not
#     handled at all. Swapping in place destroys the un-reversed colours, so 27
#     had nothing to restore even had it existed, and every erase afterwards
#     filled the screen with the inverted background -- visible the moment Node
#     started, since its REPL uses reverse video. Reverse is now state beside a
#     base attribute and `attr` is derived from the pair.
# 36: revision 36's own change is REMOVED, and the number still moves because
#     35 is on the device. Two attempts at the console background going from
#     black to a half-tone, both wrong, both taken out rather than left piled up:
#     35 blamed reverse video (a real bug in the parser, and that part stays --
#     SGR 7 without SGR 27 is simply wrong), and 36 blamed SGR leaking into the
#     window's default screen colour via update_window_config. Neither changed
#     what the device shows.
#
#     What the second attempt did establish, and what the next one should start
#     from: `set_output_info` -- SetConsoleTextAttribute -- writes
#     screen_buffer->attr and then calls update_window_config, and window.c:1848
#     copies that straight into config->attr. That is pre-existing conhost
#     behaviour with no VT involved, so the background may never have been the
#     parser's doing at all. On real Windows SetConsoleTextAttribute colours
#     subsequent writes and does not repaint the screen.
#
#     Not guessed at again without an A/B that separates the two.
# 37: located by the one question the previous two attempts should have asked
#     first -- **is a fresh cmd window affected?** It is not, which rules out
#     everything that happens before a program emits SGR, including both things
#     35 and 36 blamed. What was left is the erase.
#
#     vt_erase filled cleared cells with the *current* attribute. That is
#     "background colour erase", which xterm does and conhost does not: Windows
#     erases with the screen's default attribute. So a program that set a
#     background and then erased to end of screen painted half the console with
#     it, which is exactly the reported symptom and exactly why a fresh window
#     looked fine. Now fills with vt_default_attr.
# 38: the erase change from 37 is REVERTED, and the trace is made readable.
#     37 filled erased cells with the screen default on the theory that conhost
#     does not do background-colour erase. That is wrong: for both ED and EL the
#     background of erased cells is coloured by the current SGR state, which is
#     what terminals including conhost do. Reverted, and the reason written into
#     the code so it is not tried a third time -- if a colour appears where it
#     should not, the fault is in what SGR left in `attr`, not in the erase
#     honouring it.
#
#     37 also carried a sequence logger that produced nothing, and why is worth
#     more than the logger was: **no conhost output reaches the session log at
#     all.** A full session greps zero lines for "conhost"; the process tags
#     present are explorer.exe, fexofflinecompiler and the guest, never the
#     console host, which wineconsole spawns with its own handles. Every conhost
#     diagnostic this project has ever added was invisible. It now appends to
#     C:essel	mpt-trace.log, a DOS path through the link SessionRuntime
#     already makes for VESSEL_GFX_STATS.
# 39: the console background, diagnosed from data instead of guessed at. The
#     trace 38 shipped answered it in three lines -- Node's REPL startup is
#     `CSI 1 G`, `CSI 0 J`, `CSI 3 G` with attr=0087 throughout and **no SGR at
#     all**. Node never sets a colour, which clears both the parser and Node of
#     a bug that belonged to neither, and invalidates all three earlier fixes at
#     once, since every one of them assumed a colour was being set.
#
#     0x0087 is Vessel's own theming: PrefixRegistry writes ScreenColors 0x87,
#     background index 8. conhost's create_screen_buffer fills every cell with
#     its built-in 0x000F -- background index 0, black -- before any config is
#     read, and window.c then assigned the new attr without touching the fill.
#     The buffer therefore held two backgrounds, and the moment anything erased,
#     the region below the cursor came out a different colour from the text
#     above it. Windows repaints on a colour change; conhost did not.
#
#     Now moves the cells still carrying the old attribute, leaving deliberately
#     coloured regions alone. Pre-existing and nothing to do with VT: any
#     program that erased would have shown it, and Node is simply the first one
#     here that does.
# 40: 0052 renders 256-colour and 24-bit SGR instead of swallowing them. Revision
#     34 consumed both forms deliberately and said so -- "256/truecolour are
#     consumed and not rendered, deliberately" -- and that was the right call for
#     the programs it was built for, since Python, Node and PSReadLine colour
#     with 30-37 and 90-97. A TUI does not. Claude Code renders through Ink and
#     chalk, and the trace 38 shipped named the whole thing without guesswork:
#     253 `38;2;r;g;b`, 44 `38;5;n`, 247 `39;49` resets, and every one of the 297
#     colour sequences logged with `attr` identical before and after -- the
#     swallow visible in the data. The TUI drew its layout and none of its
#     colour, which read as a font problem because the glyphs were wrong in the
#     same session.
#
#     A cell attribute holds four bits per half, so approximating is the only
#     alternative to dropping. **Not nearest-neighbour by RGB distance**, which
#     was written first and is wrong: the sixteen are saturated primaries plus a
#     grey axis, so every muted colour is closer to a grey than to any hue --
#     `38;2;215;119;87`, a salmon accent in the trace, sits 9,408 from grey 127
#     and 21,830 from red, so a distance metric discards the hue of exactly the
#     colours a TUI uses for emphasis. Achromatic colours now go on a four-step
#     brightness ladder and the rest are classified by hue against a threshold
#     taken from the peak channel -- three quarters of it. Relative to the peak
#     rather than to the midpoint of the colour's range, and that too was
#     measured rather than chosen: Monokai's green, 166,226,46, clears the
#     midpoint on its red channel, so the midpoint rule classified it yellow and
#     would have painted diff additions cream. Checked by running the arithmetic
#     over every distinct colour in the trace and over the twelve saturated ANSI
#     colours, which all round-trip to their own index.
#
#     Half of this fix is in the APK, not here, and neither half works alone:
#     `38;5;238` is the single most common colour in the trace and quantises to
#     index 8, which PrefixRegistry had set to the console *background* so that
#     ScreenColors 0x87 could get a black background out of slot 8. Rendering it
#     correctly would have painted dim text the colour of what is behind it.
#     ScreenColors is now 0x0F -- background index 0, which is also conhost's own
#     create_screen_buffer fill, so revision 39's two-backgrounds mismatch stops
#     arising at the source and 39's repaint becomes a safety net -- and the
#     sixteen ColorTable entries are Campbell, the scheme Cascadia Mono was
#     designed alongside.
# 41: patches/wine/0053. Modern Chromium and Electron (VS Code, Discord, etc.)
#     allocate a 1.47 TB (0x15800000000) or 512 GB (0x8000000000) MEM_RESERVE
#     cage for V8 pointer-compression sandboxing. On Android ARM64 kernels with
#     39-bit VA (512 GB total user space), this request is larger than the entire
#     address space and returns STATUS_NO_MEMORY, killing the process on launch.
#     0053 clamps oversized pure MEM_RESERVE requests at base NULL to an 8 GB
#     ceiling, which easily fits in the available address space and satisfies V8.
# 43: patches/wine/0053 update. In ARM64EC translation under FEX, guest x64
#     code executes on the CHPE v2 emulator stack. The default CHPE stack was
#     hardcoded to only 256 KB (0x40000), causing instant stack exhaustion
#     (0x20980) and virtual_setup_exception stack overflow during Electron/V8
#     execution. 0053 raises chpev2_stack_size to at least 8 MB.
# 44: patches/wine/0054. Expands default kernel_stack_size from 1 MB to 4 MB
#     for Vulkan/Xlib/FEX runtime pipelines and expands 64-bit WoW64 auxiliary
#     stack from 256 KB to 1 MB.
# 45: patches/wine/0055. Two bounded readings of a stack as it is spent, because
#     42, 43 and 44 above were all spent on a theory the logs had already ruled
#     out, and the entry for 43 states it as fact. It does not hold.
#
#     Six VS Code Insiders sessions on this device span revisions 41 to 44, and
#     the overflow line is the same in all of them:
#
#       rev 41  addr 0x6fffef9c34 stack 0x20980 (0x20000-0x21000-0x820000)
#       rev 42  addr 0x6fffef9c34 stack 0x20420 (0x20000-0x21000-0x820000)
#       rev 43  addr 0x6fffef9c34 stack 0x20980 (0x20000-0x21000-0x820000)
#       rev 44  addr 0x6fffef9c34 stack 0x20980 (0x20000-0x21000-0x820000)
#
#     Rev 41 predates every one of those three commits and already shows the
#     full 8 MB range and the same death address. So 0x20980 was never evidence
#     of a 256 KB CHPE stack -- the range it sits in has been 8 MB throughout --
#     and the three revisions that raised floors moved nothing at all.
#
#     The 8 MB is not a Wine number either. `Code - Insiders.exe` carries
#     SizeOfStackReserve 0x800000 in its own PE header, the same figure Chrome,
#     Edge and Firefox ship with, so the thread already had exactly what Windows
#     would have given it. Raising a floor cannot answer a stack that is spent;
#     only reading what spent it can, which is what 0055 adds.
# 46: patches/wine/0055 rewritten after its first live reading was garbage. The
#     residue counter was Misra-Gries over 16 slots, and it (a) decremented
#     counters that were already zero, so the counts printed near 2^32, and
#     (b) could not have worked anyway: 16 slots over a million words only
#     guarantee items appearing more than n/17 times, about 62,000, while a
#     recursion 2,000 frames deep leaves each return address exactly 2,000
#     times. Wrong instrument, not just a wrong loop.
#
#     Replaced with an interval measurement, which is what a stack actually
#     offers: a recursion puts the same word at a constant stride, so the
#     commonest gap between two occurrences of one value IS the frame size, and
#     a second pass over that stride names the addresses and counts the frames.
#     Validated against synthetic 8 MB stacks with planted recursions before
#     shipping -- 3280x2000, 1360x1500, 48x100000 all recovered exactly, and
#     pure junk correctly reports that nothing repeats.
#
#     The same reading retired the other half of 0055: the per-megabyte growth
#     ladder printed nothing, because `virtual_alloc_thread_stack` maps the whole
#     stack VPROT_COMMITTED and guards only the bottom two pages. A thread stack
#     here never grows a page at a time, so there is no growth event to log. The
#     ladder was built on an assumption the source does not support and is gone.
# 47: patches/wine/0055 widened after its first real reading. VS Code Insiders
#     came back with 5,224 frames of 1,600 bytes -- the whole 8 MB -- and a
#     return address of 0x1407048b3, which is RVA 0x7048b3 in the guest image's
#     own range, four bytes past a CFG-dispatched virtual call inside a 218-byte
#     function. The recursion is in the application's x86-64 code, it is
#     unbounded rather than deep, and the same 5,240 levels would exhaust the
#     same 8 MB on Windows. No floor could ever have fixed it.
#
#     The reader named the first six distinct values it met rather than the
#     commonest, and only one was a return address. A 1,600-byte cycle holds
#     several; naming them in frequency order is what shows whether the cycle
#     crosses into Wine. Now tracks 32 and prints the top 12.
# 48: patches/wine/0056. Every SystemLink chain Wine writes ends in nothing on
#     this device: 33 chains in a container, 32 with zero resolvable entries,
#     because each names MINGLIU.TTC, SIMSUN.TTC, BATANG.TTC, MSGOTHIC.TTC and
#     SEGUISYM.TTF -- correct on Windows, absent here and unshippable. So every
#     program that is not conhost asks for a fallback glyph and is told there is
#     none, with Unifont installed and reachable from one family.
#
#     Fixed in Wine and not in the seed, which is where it was tried first and
#     failed: `update_font_system_link_info` rewrites all 32 from a hardcoded
#     table and the HACK in `update_codepage` calls it on every font init whether
#     anything changed or not, so seeded values revert inside one session. The
#     tier is appended to every value the function writes, last, where an entry
#     whose file is not found is dropped with one TRACE -- inert on a machine with
#     the real fonts, the only tier that answers on one without.
# 49: patches/wine/0055 gains a whole-frame dump. The stride passes name only
#     the words that recur at the frame interval -- the cycle's spine -- and
#     everything a frame holds that varies is invisible to them, which includes
#     the arguments and any callee reached through a shared ARM64EC signature
#     thunk. Three static attempts to identify the gdi32 function this cycle
#     calls all dead-ended (exports, .pdata, entry-thunk inversion) because it is
#     internal and reached by signature. The bytes that would say so sit in every
#     frame and were being filtered out. One frame from the middle of the
#     recursion, four words to a line, 50 lines once.
# 50: patches/wine/0057, and it is the one #57 was looking for. DirectWrite's
#     system fallback table names 59 Noto families, none present on this device,
#     so fallback_map_characters returns "success, and no font". Chromium reads
#     that as try-the-fallback-family, asks for "sans", gets the same answer, and
#     ui/gfx/platform_font_skia.cc's default-font init re-enters itself because
#     its once-flag is set only after construction -- 5,224 frames of 1,600
#     bytes, the whole 8 MB. 0057 asks the collection itself when the table
#     misses, so Unifont, which is installed and covers the BMP, can answer.
#
#     The DirectWrite half of what 0056 fixed for GDI. Skia consults neither
#     SystemLink nor FontSubstitutes, which is why four font fixes on the GDI
#     side changed nothing about this crash.
WINE_REVISION=50

# Wine Mono and Gecko: pinned and documented, deliberately not shipped.
# dlls/appwiz.cpl/addons.c looks in share/wine/{mono,gecko} before offering to
# download, so staging them there removes the dialog a new container shows.
# Measured: both 249.6 MiB, mono only 146.1 MiB, neither 66.3 MiB, and none of
# it compresses -- an .msi is a CAB already, so xz -9 took the mono payload
# from 85,504,000 to 83,246,320 bytes, 2.6%. Against that: Gecko is the
# embedded MSHTML control that engines replace with CEF, and Mono is .NET
# Framework only -- no WPF, nothing for .NET 5 and later, and Unity ships its
# own. build/wine.sh keeps the fetch helper and the four-line recipe.
WINE_MONO_VERSION=10.4.1
WINE_MONO_SHA256=071f4b2887e1c97a11d791ff3d65be9429eed6dec4c2708888bfd546ba358e23
WINE_GECKO_VERSION=2.47.4
WINE_GECKO_X86_SHA256=26cecc47706b091908f7f814bddb074c61beb8063318e9efc5a7f789857793d6
WINE_GECKO_X86_64_SHA256=e590b7d988a32d6aa4cf1d8aa3aa3d33766fdd4cf4c89c2dcc2095ecb28d066f

# --- Graphics ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Mesa/Turnip for Adreno gen8 (a829). NOT in Mesa mainline as of 2026-08:
# gallium gen8 landed in 26.0-devel, Vulkan is still out-of-tree.
# This branch moves fast and has no tags — the build records the exact SHA
# into the .wcp metadata so any build is reproducible after the fact.
MESA_REPO=https://github.com/whitebelyash/mesa-unified.git
MESA_REF=turnip/gen8

# Prefer pinning to a known-good commit over tracking the branch head.
#
# Community reference: StevenMXZ "Turnip Gen8" V33, built from this same
# whitebelyash turnip/gen8 branch plus Mesa upstream. It lists explicit support
# for a840/a830/a829/a825/810 and reports Vulkan 1.4.354. V33 is confirmed
# working on this device, so it is the behaviour our build should match or beat.
#   Release: https://github.com/StevenMXZ/freedreno_turnip-CI/releases (tag v33)
#   Build recipe: StevenMXZ/Adreno-Tools-Drivers @ A8xx, build_turnip.sh
#
# Note: the "50cbd61" shown on that release page is a commit in the *CI* repo
# (the build scripts), not a Mesa commit — it identifies the recipe, not the
# source. The Mesa SHA has to come from the CI run log or from the built
# driver itself:
#   strings libvulkan_freedreno.so | grep -iE 'mesa [0-9]|git-[0-9a-f]{7,}'
#
# Leave empty to build the branch head; set it to freeze a reproducible build.
# Pinned 2026-08-07. The branch head moved under us mid-session (9c51ede5 ->
# 9c475fc3) and both Mesa components silently changed version, which is exactly
# what a moving ref does. 9c475fc3 is the commit Turnip and Zink were both
# verified against. Clear this to track the head again.
MESA_SHA=9c475fc3

# Vessel's patch revision on top of that Mesa commit -- see FEX_REVISION for the
# failure this exists to prevent. Turnip's version code is derived from the Mesa
# version string alone, so without this a rebuild carrying a new patch produces
# the code already in the store and the old build keeps being served.
# 2: patches/mesa/0008, the dma-heap the swapchain buffer comes from. Two things
#    kept it off the device and both are worth writing down, because either
#    alone looks exactly like a patch that did not work. .github/workflows/
#    turnip.yml watched `patches/turnip/**`, a directory that does not exist --
#    Turnip's patches are in patches/mesa/, as build/turnip.sh's SOURCE_NAME
#    says and as zink.yml already had right -- so the patch triggered no build.
#    And had it built, the version code is derived from the Mesa version string
#    alone, so at revision 1 it would have installed as bytes the store already
#    had. The filter is fixed in the same commit as this bump.
TURNIP_REVISION=2

# Zink — desktop OpenGL over Vulkan, built from Mesa as an ARM64EC PE
# opengl32.dll (see build/zink.sh for why a native opengl32.dll rather than
# winex11.drv). It is built from the SAME checkout as Turnip above, so it has
# no ref of its own and MESA_REF versions both.
#
# These two exist only as an escape hatch. The Turnip tree is a fork carrying
# out-of-tree freedreno gen8 work and nothing in its CI builds for Windows, so
# it is possible for a rebase there to break the WGL path while Turnip itself
# is fine. Setting ZINK_MESA_REF moves the Zink build to its own checkout at
# native/mesa-zink (upstream Mesa unless ZINK_MESA_REPO says otherwise) and
# leaves Turnip untouched. Also update ref_var in .github/workflows/zink.yml
# if you set it, or CI will keep watching MESA_REF.
#
# Leave both empty to share the Turnip tree, which is the intended state.
ZINK_MESA_REPO=
ZINK_MESA_REF=

#     ARM64X: rebuilt as a hybrid image so a native ARM64 process can load it.
#     A pure ARM64EC DLL declares machine 0x8664 and a classic ARM64 loader
#     refuses it with STATUS_INVALID_IMAGE_FORMAT, which left every native
#     ARM64 Windows program with no graphics stack at all. docs/ARM64X.md.
ZINK_REVISION=1

# libadrenotools — the only reason a Mesa driver can be loaded on Android at all.
# Turnip builds as an Android Vulkan HAL (one exported symbol, HMI), so the
# platform loader is the only thing that can load it, and it will only load a
# driver out of a vendor namespace. libadrenotools interposes android_dlopen_ext
# so the driver can come from app storage instead. See
# docs/ARCHITECTURE.md#turnip-does-need-adrenotools.
#
# VENDORED, NOT FETCHED — unlike everything else in this file. It ships inside
# the APK rather than as a .wcp, because its hook objects must live in
# `applicationInfo.nativeLibraryDir` and nowhere else works; the reasoning is in
# app/src/main/cpp/adrenotools/README.md. No build script reads these two values
# and there is no ADRENOTOOLS_REF for CI to watch. They are here because this
# file is where the question "what version of X does Vessel build?" is answered,
# and an exception that is not written down is a version nobody can find.
#
# Updating it means re-copying the source into app/src/main/cpp/adrenotools/ and
# changing the commit here and in that directory's README.
ADRENOTOOLS_REPO=https://github.com/bylaws/libadrenotools.git
ADRENOTOOLS_SHA=8fae8ce
ADRENOTOOLS_LINKERNSBYPASS_SHA=aa39758

DXVK_REPO=https://github.com/doitsujin/dxvk.git
DXVK_REF=v2.7.1
# Vessel's patch revision on top of that tag -- see FEX_REVISION. This did not
# exist until build/dxvk.sh grew a --version-code; every DXVK build before that
# shipped as the bare 20701 that package_wcp.py derives from "2.7.1" alone, so
# a rebuild carrying a changed patch installed as a no-op. Any revision here
# produces 2070100 + revision, which is far above 20701, so 1 is already
# unambiguous against everything that has shipped.
# 1: patches/dxvk/0001 gains the GPU and CS idle/sync tick counters.
#     ARM64X: rebuilt as a hybrid image so a native ARM64 process can load it.
#     A pure ARM64EC DLL declares machine 0x8664 and a classic ARM64 loader
#     refuses it with STATUS_INVALID_IMAGE_FORMAT, which left every native
#     ARM64 Windows program with no graphics stack at all. docs/ARM64X.md.
DXVK_REVISION=2

VKD3D_REPO=https://github.com/HansKristian-Work/vkd3d-proton.git
VKD3D_REF=v3.0.1
# Vessel's patch revision on top of that tag -- see FEX_REVISION.
# 1: patches/vkd3d/0001, the per-shader DXBC header chatter demoted to TRACE.
# 7: patches/vkd3d/0007, the per-frame D3D12 counters the host's panel graphs.
# 8: 0007's comment corrected. It asserted that "Z: is '/' in every Wine prefix,
#    so [the rewrite] is a no-op where DXVK is right and the fix where it is
#    lucky" -- and Vessel's DriveMap.removeRootDrive removes Z: from every
#    prefix on purpose, so the rewrite named a drive that cannot resolve. DXVK's
#    plain fopen was not right either. Both producers failed the open in
#    silence and the panel reported "no Direct3D counters" for a title that was
#    rendering. The fix is host-side (GFX_STATS_DOS_PATH) and needs no new
#    binary, so this revision buys only a comment that stops the next reader
#    believing the same thing -- but a patch file that changed while the code
#    did not is exactly the case the store cannot tell apart, so it bumps.
# 9: patches/vkd3d/0008. re9.exe added to the application quirk table with
#    re4_quirks, i.e. FORCE_MIN16_AS_32BIT. vkd3d already carries this for
#    re2.exe, re7.exe and re4.exe -- "lots of shaders which cause random
#    flicker due to bad 16-bit behavior; these shaders really need 32-bit to
#    render properly" -- and re9.exe matched nothing, so it inherited none of
#    it. On Turnip the failure is not flicker but missing geometry: a vertex
#    shader whose position math overflows fp16 yields Inf or NaN and the GPU
#    discards those triangles, which drops specific meshes, leaves everything
#    around them drawing, and logs nothing. That is docs/TODO.md #56 exactly.
#    Reached only after every driver-side flag was eliminated on the device --
#    none of which can tell one mesh from another, where a shader quirk can.
#    Unverified, and honestly so: re_hashes are RE2/RE4 shader hashes and will
#    not match this game, so what actually applies is the global 32-bit half.
# 10: patches/vkd3d/0007, regenerated. ExecuteIndirect's DGC-skip return
#     (command.c ~17176-17181) used to discard the whole call behind a GPU
#     debug-marker label nobody reads without a capture -- now a FIXME_ONCE
#     immediately above the return names what was dropped: the byte offset
#     (`argument_buffer_offset_for_command`) and the command count
#     (`max_command_count`). That return is reachable only for a command
#     signature that would have needed the compute-shader state template, and
#     on this device it never can: EXT_dgc is entirely unimplemented in
#     Turnip, zero matches across native/mesa/src/freedreno, so
#     `requires_state_template` can never be true and the
#     "Not all relevant pipeline stages are supported by EXT_dgc" line vkd3d
#     prints every session is not scene-specific evidence for #56 -- it fires
#     unconditionally. For a plain draw-only signature the fallback vkd3d
#     actually takes is a direct vkCmdDrawIndirect / vkCmdDrawIndexedIndirect /
#     *IndirectCount call, not the compute-patching pass the header comment in
#     vkd3d_vessel_stats.h used to claim; that comment is corrected in the same
#     commit, which is why the patch file changed and the revision moves. No
#     counters or JSON schema changed, so the host side needs nothing.
# 11: patches/vkd3d/0008 rewritten. The fp16 half was tried on the device and
#     lost -- re9.exe ran against it and the walls were still missing -- so the
#     entry now carries FORCE_COMPUTE_BARRIER alone instead. 0007's counters gave
#     the reason: 196 ExecuteIndirects and 195.2 dispatches per frame, one
#     compute pass per indirect draw, which is GPU-driven culling. A CPU counter
#     cannot see whether the culling shader's count reached the draw, so a
#     missing UAV -> indirect-args barrier loses the geometry while the counter
#     still reads 196. Upstream carries the same quirk for the same shape in
#     Witcher 3 and Horizon Forbidden West.
# 12: patches/vkd3d/0008 deleted. Both halves were tried on the device against
#     the scene that is missing its walls and both lost: FORCE_MIN16_AS_32BIT
#     first, then FORCE_COMPUTE_BARRIER alone, the latter verified running by
#     comparing the prefix's d3d12core.dll against the build byte for byte. A
#     quirk that has been tested and lost is worse than none -- it costs 32-bit
#     math or forced barriers on every shader and makes the next reading
#     impossible to attribute. #56 keeps the eliminations; the tree keeps no
#     RE9 quirk.
#     ARM64X: rebuilt as a hybrid image so a native ARM64 process can load it.
#     A pure ARM64EC DLL declares machine 0x8664 and a classic ARM64 loader
#     refuses it with STATUS_INVALID_IMAGE_FORMAT, which left every native
#     ARM64 Windows program with no graphics stack at all. docs/ARM64X.md.
VKD3D_REVISION=13

# --- Wine's Android dependencies -----------------------------------------------
#
# The NDK ships no X11 and no FreeType, so build/x11-sysroot.sh cross-builds them
# into a staging sysroot that Wine's unix side links against. These are upstream
# release tarballs, not git refs, so they are pinned by version string; the URLs
# are assembled in x11-sysroot.sh from these values.
#
# Every one of these is the newest release that still ships an autotools
# configure script — verified 2026-08-07 by unpacking each tarball. X.Org is
# migrating to meson, and several of these (xorgproto, libXau, libXfixes,
# libXrandr, libXcomposite, libXxf86vm, libXinerama) already carry a meson.build
# alongside. When one of them drops configure entirely, either pin the last
# autotools release or teach x11-sysroot.sh to use meson for that package;
# the script fails loudly on a missing configure rather than skipping it.
#
# Bumping any value here invalidates the sysroot stamp and rebuilds the stack.
X11_UTIL_MACROS=1.20.2
X11_XORGPROTO=2025.1
X11_XTRANS=1.6.0
X11_LIBXAU=1.0.12
X11_XCBPROTO=1.17.0
X11_PTHREAD_STUBS=0.5
X11_LIBXCB=1.17.0
# libxshmfence is not Wine's dependency — it is Mesa's. The X11 WSI in
# wsi_x11.c uses a shared-memory fence per DRI3 buffer to know when the server
# has finished with it, so `-Dplatforms=x11` will not configure without
# xshmfence.pc. Newest release; still autotools (verified by unpacking).
X11_LIBXSHMFENCE=1.3.3
X11_LIBX11=1.8.13
X11_LIBXEXT=1.3.7
X11_LIBXRENDER=0.9.12
X11_LIBXFIXES=6.0.2
X11_LIBXI=1.8.3
X11_LIBXRANDR=1.5.5
X11_LIBXCURSOR=1.2.3
X11_LIBXCOMPOSITE=0.4.7
X11_LIBXXF86VM=1.1.7
X11_LIBXINERAMA=1.1.6

# GnuTLS and its dependencies, also not X11 and also sharing this sysroot.
#
# Wine's bcrypt has no asymmetric crypto without them, and it fails silently:
# dlls/bcrypt/gnutls.c is the entire unix backend and is wrapped in
# `#ifdef HAVE_GNUTLS_CIPHER_INIT`, so a configure that does not find GnuTLS
# produces a bcrypt.so that returns STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED from every
# asymmetric call and logs "no encryption support". Titles doing licence or
# anti-tamper work through BCryptCreateKeyPair get that and generally crash.
#
# Order matters: gmp, then nettle (whose hogweed half needs gmp and is the part
# GnuTLS uses), then libtasn1, then GnuTLS itself.
#
# All four are LGPL and ship in the .wcp, so they belong in the source offer
# alongside the X11 set.
#
# gmp is the one of the four that must be built SHARED, and the version is not
# what carries that -- build/x11-sysroot.sh's configure flags are. bcrypt gates
# Diffie-Hellman on `HAVE_GMP_H && SONAME_LIBGMP` (dlls/bcrypt/gnutls.c:653) and
# dlopen()s the soname at runtime, so a static libgmp.a leaves SONAME_LIBGMP
# undefined and every session logs "Compiled without DH support." The full
# mechanism, with the config.log lines, is on the gmp entry in that script.
GMP_VERSION=6.3.0
# 3.10, and it must be at least 3.10 -- that is the release that added
# rsa_oaep_sha256_encrypt, which is the function GnuTLS's configure tests to
# decide whether to compile its own RSA-OAEP backport:
#
#   configure.ac:793
#   AM_CONDITIONAL([NEED_RSA_OAEP],
#     [test "$ac_cv_func_nettle_rsa_oaep_sha256_encrypt" != yes])
#
# That backport (lib/nettle/Makefile.am:117-131) includes rsa-sign-tr.c, which
# defines nettle_rsa_compute_root_tr -- a symbol nettle already exports. Compile
# both and the link dies:
#
#   ld.lld: error: duplicate symbol: nettle_rsa_compute_root_tr
#   >>> rsa-sign-tr.o in archive nettle/.libs/libcrypto.a
#
# The check is AC_CHECK_FUNC, which does not survive a cross build, so configure
# concludes the function is absent however new nettle is. build/x11-sysroot.sh
# therefore forces the cache variable on GnuTLS's configure line. Going back to
# 3.9.1 was tried and is strictly worse: that release really does lack the
# function, so the backport is genuinely needed and the collision is unavoidable.
NETTLE_VERSION=3.10
LIBTASN1_VERSION=4.19.0
GNUTLS_VERSION=3.8.4
GNUTLS_SERIES=3.8

# FreeType is not X11 but shares the same sysroot: Wine's configure treats a
# missing FreeType as a hard error, and a Wine without it has no glyph
# rasterizer at all.
FREETYPE_VERSION=2.14.3

# --- GStreamer, and the Media Foundation backend it is ------------------------
#
# Wine's whole media stack is winegstreamer. mfplat, mfreadwrite, mf, mfsrcsnk
# and the DirectShow parsers are PE modules that do format negotiation and
# nothing else; every demux and every decode happens in winegstreamer.so on the
# unix side. Build Wine --without-gstreamer and those PE DLLs still install, so
# the tree *looks* complete -- and then
#
#   MFCreateSourceReaderFromByteStream -> 0xC00D36C4  MF_E_UNSUPPORTED_BYTESTREAM_TYPE
#   ole:com_get_class_object class {317df618-5e5a-468a-9f15-d827a9a08162} not registered
#
# That CLSID is CLSID_GStreamerByteStreamHandler
# (dlls/winegstreamer/winegstreamer_classes.idl). It is the only byte stream
# handler Wine has for anything that is not a raw WAV or MP3, so with
# winegstreamer absent every media open fails and titles that gate on their
# intro video stop on a modal dialog. Measured with Resident Evil Requiem.
#
# configure.ac:1731 names the pkg-config modules, and all five must resolve or
# WINE_PACKAGE_FLAGS leaves GSTREAMER_CFLAGS empty and skips the rest of the
# check silently:
#
#   gstreamer-1.0 gstreamer-video-1.0 gstreamer-audio-1.0
#   gstreamer-tag-1.0 gstreamer-gl-1.0
#
# gstreamer-gl-1.0 is not optional and not a mistake: unixlib.c and wg_parser.c
# both #include <gst/gl/gl.h>. It is a build-time dependency only --
# gst_gl_display_new() failing at runtime is a GST_ERROR, not a fatal.
#
# The GStreamer modules are one release, always. Mixing 1.28 core with 1.26
# plugins is not supported upstream and the failure is a plugin that loads and
# then reports no features.
GSTREAMER_VERSION=1.28.6

# GLib is GStreamer's whole object system, and it needs these two. Both are
# glib meson subprojects when absent, which would make the build fetch from the
# network mid-run -- so they are pinned and built here instead.
#
# pcre2 is built --disable-jit deliberately. PCRE2's JIT wants an anonymous
# PROT_EXEC mapping, which is the same execmod restriction that shapes how this
# project loads everything else on Android, and GRegex is not on any path where
# the JIT would pay for the risk.
LIBFFI_VERSION=3.4.8
PCRE2_VERSION=10.45
GLIB_VERSION=2.88.3
GLIB_SERIES=2.88

# The third GLib dependency, and the one that is not obvious: bionic has no
# gettext and no libintl at all, and `-Dnls=disabled` does NOT make GLib stop
# needing one -- meson.build:2352 asks for the `intl` dependency unconditionally
# and falls straight through to its subproject:
#
#   Run-time dependency intl found: NO  (tried builtin and system)
#   Looking for a fallback subproject for the dependency intl
#   ERROR: Subproject proxy-libintl is buildable: NO
#   meson.build:2352:12: ERROR: Automatic wrap-based subproject downloading is disabled
#
# proxy-libintl is the stub GLib's own subprojects/proxy-libintl.wrap names, and
# 0.5 is the exact version and tarball that wrap points at -- sha256
# f7a1cbd7579baaf575c66f9d99fb6295e9b0684a28b095967cfda17857595303, verified
# against the wrap file in the GLib tarball. Building it explicitly rather than
# letting meson download it keeps the rule this file exists for: everything
# built is answerable from this repository, and no build reaches the network for
# a source it did not pin.
PROXY_LIBINTL_VERSION=0.5

# FFmpeg, reached only through gst-libav. This is the H.264 and AAC decoder --
# without it the plugin set can demux an MP4 and then has nothing to hand the
# video track to, which meets the letter of "winegstreamer builds" and none of
# the point of it.
#
# LGPL, not GPL: no --enable-gpl and no --enable-nonfree. Wine is LGPL-2.1 and
# so is the rest of this payload; enabling GPL components would change the
# license of the package. It also costs nothing we want -- the GPL pieces here
# are x264/x265 encoders and the yadif deinterlacer, and GStreamer does its own
# deinterlacing.
#
# --target-os=android is load-bearing beyond the obvious. FFmpeg's configure
# has an explicit android case that sets SLIB_INSTALL_NAME='$(SLIBNAME)' and
# SLIB_INSTALL_LINKS='', i.e. UNVERSIONED sonames -- libavcodec.so, not
# libavcodec.so.62. Everything else in this payload is unversioned too (bionic
# has no versioned-symlink chain and build/wine.sh copies *.so by glob), so a
# linux target here would ship a libavcodec.so that no NEEDED entry names.
FFMPEG_VERSION=8.0.3

# --- Tools: the developer toolchain inside the container -----------------------
#
# One `Tools` component carrying Git, Python, Node.js, PowerShell 7 and a JDK.
# Not five packages, and the reason is structural rather than a preference: a
# container references exactly one component per type (ComponentStore.kt,
# `referencesOf` returns a type -> versionCode map with no list in it), and
# `Tools` is one type, so
# `adoptLatest` moves that single reference to whichever package has the highest
# code. Publishing Python as its own `Tools` package would therefore not add
# Python — it would silently *replace* Git, copy a Python tree into
# C:\Program Files\Git, and leave a PATH pointing at directories that no longer
# exist. docs/DEVTOOLS.md §7.3 walks that failure through step by step.
#
# **Nothing here is built. Every one of these is an upstream release archive,
# verified and repackaged**, which is why they are pinned by URL and sha256
# rather than by git ref, and why build/tools.sh calls neither setup_ndk nor
# setup_mingw. There is no compiler in this component's build.
#
# **All five are ARM64, and 1.2.0 is a revert rather than a new experiment.**
# Vessel runs Wine ARM64EC, so an ARM64 Windows PE runs natively and an x86-64
# one goes through FEX. The component that originally shipped was ARM64
# (dist/git-2.55.0.3-arm64.wcp); 1.1.0 made the whole payload x86-64 on one
# claim — that PyPI resolves far more `win_amd64` wheels than `win_arm64` — and
# that claim has been measured and is false.
#
# Counted against PyPI on 2026-08-17, wheels published per release:
#
#     numpy 2.5.2            win_arm64 6   win_amd64 6
#     pandas 3.0.5           win_arm64 5   win_amd64 5
#     pillow 12.3.0          win_arm64 8   win_amd64 9
#     lxml 6.1.1             win_arm64 7   win_amd64 9
#     pydantic-core 2.48.0   win_arm64 7   win_amd64 9
#     cryptography 50.0.0    win_arm64 0   win_amd64 4
#
# That is parity. **cryptography is the one genuine gap and it is the known cost
# of this decision**: it publishes no `win_arm64` wheel at all, and with no C
# compiler in the prefix there is no sdist fallback, so `pip install cryptography`
# — and anything that depends on it, which is a long list — fails on this payload.
# It fails at install time with an error rather than running slowly, which is the
# better failure of the two, but it is a real one and it is named here so nobody
# has to rediscover it.
#
# **The measurement that forced the revert is a crash, not a benchmark.**
# PowerShell 7.6.5 x86-64 fails reproducibly on the device the moment .NET does
# real work: two unhandled `c0000005` access violations at 0x6f9d20dbec and
# 0x6f9d22ad05 — the same region, and inside no module Wine logged, which puts
# them in FEX's JIT buffer rather than in any Wine DLL. It is not connectivity:
# `ping google.com` in the same prefix resolves DNS and gets 4/4 replies. So the
# fault is .NET under FEX. ARM64 does not work around it; it deletes the layer the
# fault lives in.
#
# **Whether that fixes it is unverified.** No ARM64 tree in this payload has been
# run on the device. "The crash goes away" is the expectation behind 1.2.0, not a
# result of it.
#
# The JDK is where the switch is worth the most rather than costing anything: see
# TOOLS_JAVA_VERSION, which used to record the cost of the x64 choice.
#
# **Git is mixed-architecture in BOTH builds, so that is not an argument either
# way.** Measured by reading IMAGE_FILE_HEADER.Machine off six PEs of the ARM64
# tree: `cmd/git.exe`, `bin/git.exe` and `clangarm64/bin/git.exe` are 0xAA64,
# while `usr/bin/bash.exe`, `usr/bin/ls.exe` and `usr/bin/msys-2.0.dll` are
# 0x8664 — there is no ARM64 port of msys-2.0, as docs/DEVTOOLS.md §7.1 first
# recorded. What ARM64 buys is a native `git.exe`, the binary anything actually
# invokes. It does not make Git Bash native and it does not touch the `fork()`
# emulation TerminalProfile.kt:56-70 measured pegging a core at 98% — PowerShell
# is still the answer to that, which is why it is in the payload.
#
# The ARM64 tree uses a `clangarm64` prefix where the x64 one uses `mingw64` —
# see PrefixRegistry.toolsPath and its `MSYSTEM`, which move together with this
# and are the half of the change that is easy to get half-right.
#
# Zips and self-extracting archives only, never installers. docs/TODO.md #17
# records a measured, open, unexplained `.msi` failure in this container:
# msiexec draws its window and the payload never lands in C:\Program Files. Every
# one of these five ships a portable archive, so nothing here needs msiexec and
# nothing here runs on the device — extraction and pip bootstrap are build-host
# steps. PowerShell publishes both a `.msi` and a `.zip` and Temurin publishes
# both a `.msi` and a `.zip`; the zip is taken in both cases for exactly that
# reason.
#
# The version below is Vessel's own, not any component's: six upstream payloads
# cannot be folded into one dotted string that still orders correctly. Bump the
# minor when the contents change and TOOLS_REVISION when only the packaging does.
# The code build/tools.sh passes is the literal 1500000, and it is a literal
# rather than derived from this string on purpose: package_wcp.py's version_code
# would turn "1.5.0" into 10500 and the revision multiply makes that 1,050,000 —
# which is BELOW 1.4.0's 1,400,000 and would therefore be adopted as older than
# what is installed. The derivation was already unpredictable at 1.1.0; from 1.2.0
# on it is also wrong. The floor is what is actually on the device, 1,400,000 --
# anything at or under that is adopted as no newer than what is already installed
# and the build looks exactly like a package that did not install. See
# FEX_REVISION for that failure mode in general.
#
# TOOLS_REVISION is the two low digits and build/tools.sh asserts it is 0, since
# the literal above does not read it.
#
# **1.5.0 and not 1.4.0, because 1.4.0 is on the device right now.** The component
# store is keyed by type and version code and nothing else: WcpInstaller.kt:290-305
# does not unpack a package whose type+versionCode pair it already has, so adding
# the two Unifont files back under the same 1400000 would build fine, install fine,
# and leave the phone with a `windows\Fonts` holding Cascadia alone — the
# SystemLink value would name two files that are not there, every entry would be
# discarded with a `TRACE` line (font.c:2076), and the console would render exactly
# as it does today with nothing anywhere saying why. The history of this number:
#
#   1.1.0 / 1100000  the five-tree bundle, all x86-64.
#   1.2.0 / 1200000  the same five trees rebuilt ARM64 — the wheel-ecosystem
#                    claim behind 1.1.0 was counted against PyPI and is false,
#                    and PowerShell x86-64 crashed on the device.
#   1.3.0 / 1300000  adds Fonts/ — GNU Unifont. The prefix had no fonts at all,
#                    so a TUI's box-drawing came out as `□□□□□`.
#   1.4.0 / 1400000  replaces Unifont with Cascadia Mono, below. Unifont worked
#                    and was still dropped, for legibility; the coverage that
#                    goes with it is named on that pin. A contents change, so
#                    the minor moves.
#   1.5.0 / 1500000  puts Unifont back *beside* Cascadia rather than instead of
#                    it, because the choice 1.3.0 and 1.4.0 were each making —
#                    legible or complete — was never the only one on offer.
#                    Windows resolves this per glyph through GDI font linking and
#                    Wine implements it; see TOOLS_UNIFONT_VERSION below for the
#                    whole path, read in the tree we ship. Cascadia stays the
#                    face, Unifont becomes what it falls back to. A contents
#                    change, so the minor moves.
#
# The superseded dist/tools-1.4.0-arm64.wcp is deleted rather than left beside the
# new one — see app/build.gradle.kts on why the bill of materials is named and not
# globbed.
TOOLS_VERSION=1.5.0
TOOLS_REVISION=0

# Git for Windows, the portable self-extracting 7z, ARM64 asset:
# PortableGit-2.55.0.4-arm64.7z.exe, 59,191,568 bytes. Verified 2026-08-17: this
# is the newest release, and the sha256 is the one git-for-windows publishes in
# the release body's Filename/SHA-256 table rather than one computed here, so a
# mismatch means the asset moved and not that the download was truncated. (The
# 64-bit asset in the same table is 016e8423…; that is the one 1.1.0 pinned and it
# is not this.)
#
# `-arm64` and not `-64-bit`, and the tree that comes out has a `clangarm64`
# prefix where the 64-bit one has `mingw64`. build/tools.sh asserts
# `clangarm64/bin` exists and PrefixRegistry.toolsPath names it on PATH with
# MSYSTEM=CLANGARM64; all three move together or the install finds `git.exe`
# through `cmd\` and misses every helper behind it.
TOOLS_GIT_VERSION=2.55.0.4
TOOLS_GIT_TAG=v2.55.0.windows.4
TOOLS_GIT_SHA256=d69d0c6a3c5445553565ef74f1d9e22a9869f57c246111db347dd96c252b4da5

# CPython's Windows embeddable package, which is the answer to "installer or
# portable?" and not a workaround for it: it is a flat directory of python.exe,
# python314.dll, the stdlib as python314.zip and the .pyd extension modules,
# published by CPython to be unzipped inside another application.
#
# The ARM64 asset, python-3.14.7-embed-arm64.zip, 11,980,260 bytes, HTTP 200 on
# 2026-08-17. **The sha256 is python.org's own published value, not one computed
# here** — the 3.14.7 release page's files table has a SHA-256 column (and no MD5
# column any more) and lists f6773983… against "Windows embeddable package
# (ARM64)". A download here computed the same value, so this is a pin and a
# mismatch means the asset moved. (The 64-bit row in that same table is
# d297e5ff…, which is what 1.1.0 pinned.)
#
# It ships `site` disabled through python314._pth and carries no pip.
# build/tools.sh fixes both on the build host — see the get-pip pin below. Both
# fixes are architecture-independent: the `_pth` edit is a text edit, and pip's
# own wheel is `py3-none-any`, so nothing about the ARM64 switch touches them.
TOOLS_PYTHON_VERSION=3.14.7
TOOLS_PYTHON_SHA256=f6773983c8959d4281e48c4540cb0bdd23e42391e4e951ce17e7ceb52658f21c

# Node.js, the plain zip rather than the .msi, ARM64: node-v26.7.0-win-arm64.zip,
# 36,809,093 bytes, HTTP 200 on 2026-08-17. sha256 read off nodejs.org's own
# SHASUMS256.txt for the release — published upstream, so it is a pin. It unpacks
# to node-v<ver>-win-arm64/, which build/tools.sh flattens so node.exe sits at the
# payload subdirectory root and neither the version nor the architecture reaches
# PATH.
#
# npm and npx come along as `.cmd` shims over a pure-JavaScript node_modules/npm,
# so nothing in the npm layer is architecture-specific. What is architecture-
# specific is what npm *installs*: packages with prebuilt native binaries ship
# `win32-x64` more widely than `win32-arm64`, which is the npm half of the wheel
# argument above. It is a real narrowing, unmeasured here — the PyPI numbers were
# counted, this was not — and it does not outweigh a reproducible crash.
TOOLS_NODE_VERSION=26.7.0
TOOLS_NODE_SHA256=be8775204cfceca5a73c30f91bf0de5e85274c01b776dc13f16b91aa251ebb01

# PowerShell 7, and it is here for one specific program rather than for shell
# taste. docs/DEVTOOLS.md §4 works out that Claude Code executes commands through
# exactly two tools, and that both are broken in this container: its Bash tool
# means Git Bash, and TerminalProfile.kt:56-70 measured `bash --login -i` here as
# a child process pegging a core at 98% in MSYS2's `fork()` emulation with the
# parent waiting on a handshake that never lands; its PowerShell tool falls back
# to Wine's `powershell.exe`, which is a stub. TerminalProfile.kt:38-45 already
# named this as the answer — *"PowerShell 7 cannot be built from source and
# belongs behind the component downloader"* — and this is that, moved into the
# bundled payload because a toolchain that needs a manual pick is one nobody has.
#
# **Self-contained .NET, so it needs nothing else in the prefix.** The win-arm64
# zip carries its own CoreCLR as a native ARM64 PE. It does not want wine-mono,
# which this build deliberately does not ship (build/wine.sh:635-697), and it
# does not want a runtime installed first — which matters here because installing
# one would mean an `.msi`, and TODO #17 is exactly that failing.
#
# **This is the tree the 1.2.0 architecture switch was made for.** The x86-64 zip
# of this exact version crashed on the device, twice, reproducibly: unhandled
# `c0000005` at 0x6f9d20dbec and again at 0x6f9d22ad05, the moment .NET did real
# work — same address region, inside no module Wine logged, i.e. in FEX's JIT
# buffer. `ping google.com` in the same prefix got 4/4 replies, so it is not the
# network. build/tools.sh asserts coreclr.dll's Machine word is 0xAA64 for exactly
# this reason: an x86-64 CoreCLR reaching the device again is that crash reaching
# the device again. Whether the ARM64 build gets further is UNVERIFIED — nothing
# has run it.
#
# **Unverified: PSReadLine may render as literal escape sequences.** PSReadLine
# is VT-heavy and Wine's conhost has no VT parser, so PowerShell's own probe of
# ENABLE_VIRTUAL_TERMINAL_PROCESSING gets a yes it should get a no.
# patches/wine/0052 stops conhost claiming the capability, which should push
# PowerShell onto its Console API rendering path, but that patch is in flight and
# neither half of this has been measured on the device. Until then `pwsh` in a
# window may print `[?25l[1;35m` down the screen instead of a prompt. Note this
# is a rendering question, not an "does it run" question — nothing here says
# whether a self-contained CoreCLR starts under FEX at all, which is still the
# highest-value unknown in docs/DEVTOOLS.md.
#
# Verified 2026-08-17: v7.6.5 is the newest release and
# PowerShell-7.6.5-win-arm64.zip is 99,526,416 bytes, HTTP 200. **The sha256 is
# published** — the release body's hash table lists it against the arm64 asset (in
# uppercase there; lowercased here to match sha256sum's output) and a download
# computed the same value, so this is a pin rather than a record of what
# downloaded. (The x64 asset is 106,319,290 bytes at 32eb8f6c…, which is what
# 1.1.0 pinned.) It unpacks flat, exactly as the x64 zip did, so build/tools.sh
# moves the whole thing to Pwsh/ rather than stripping a wrapper directory the way
# Node's zip needs.
TOOLS_PWSH_VERSION=7.6.5
TOOLS_PWSH_SHA256=20514a755d16428dc4355c85e0883c859531e71cc3e122670aa1fccdbf96ba7e

# Eclipse Temurin JDK 21 (LTS), HotSpot. The `+` is part of the upstream build
# identifier and build/tools.sh derives all three spellings of it from this one
# string — `jdk-21.0.12+8` the directory, `21.0.12_8` the archive name, and
# `jdk-21.0.12%2B8` the release tag in the URL.
#
# **aarch64 and not x64, and this is where the 1.2.0 switch pays the most.**
# 1.1.0 took the x86-64 JDK deliberately, for payload consistency, and wrote the
# cost down: it was JIT-on-JIT. HotSpot generates machine code at runtime and then
# rewrites it, and FEX then has to translate code that did not exist when the
# process started and that changes underneath it. That is FEX's hardest path and
# most of what the FEX_REVISION history at the top of this file is about — the SMC
# handler faulting on a page whose protection was raised by DEP promotion and
# re-faulting 5,668,864 times, the OvercommitTracker's RWX reprotect loop,
# DisableDEP and the CodeInvalidationMutex deadlocks around it. That comment ended
# "an ARM64 JDK would have sidestepped every one of those"; this is that JDK, and
# every one of those paths is now off the table for Java. There was never a
# constraint pushing the other way — bytecode is bytecode and there is no
# `win_arm64` wheel to be missing.
#
# **Whether HotSpot starts here is still unverified**, and the switch does not
# change that: nothing on the device has run a JVM under either architecture.
# Treat "there is a JDK in the payload" and "Java works on the phone" as two
# separate claims, only the first of which this build establishes. What can be
# said is narrower and worth saying: the specific failure mode 1.1.0 named as the
# risk is no longer on the path.
#
# Verified 2026-08-17 against the Adoptium API
# (api.adoptium.net/v3/assets/latest/21/hotspot?architecture=aarch64&image_type=jdk
# &os=windows&vendor=eclipse): release jdk-21.0.12+8,
# OpenJDK21U-jdk_aarch64_windows_hotspot_21.0.12_8.zip, 192,677,972 bytes, HTTP
# 200. The sha256 is `binary.package.checksum` straight out of that response —
# **published by Adoptium**, so like Git's, Node's, Python's and PowerShell's it is
# a pin and not a record of what downloaded; a download here computed the same
# value. (The x64 binary in the same release is 205,069,442 bytes at 9ba963ee…,
# which is what 1.1.0 pinned.)
#
# Note the spelling: Adoptium says `aarch64` where the other four upstreams say
# `arm64`. It unpacks to jdk-21.0.12+8/ — the same directory name for both
# architectures — which build/tools.sh flattens for the same reason it flattens
# Node's: a version string inside the tree becomes a version string in PATH, and
# every bump then needs a registry seed change.
TOOLS_JAVA_VERSION=21.0.12+8
TOOLS_JAVA_SHA256=e7ddf1cf45885cae2cfb8a018db60b0041ef69e96843b93a92b447d9a4cba676

# **The one pin here whose URL has no version in it.** get-pip.py is served from
# a single unversioned path that pypa rewrites whenever pip releases, so this
# sha256 is the only thing that says which bootstrap actually ran, and it is the
# only pin in this file expected to go stale on its own — nothing upstream moved
# when it does. The value is what the URL served on 2026-08-17, 2,230,488 bytes,
# computed here because pypa publishes no checksum for it.
#
# A mismatch is not a security event and should not be read as one: re-download,
# check the file still starts with pip's own bootstrap header, and update this
# line. It fails the build rather than warning because a bootstrap nobody chose
# is a payload nobody can reproduce.
TOOLS_GET_PIP_SHA256=fb24e693bab954209a063d90953621412ccad4a500905a726286e038f508ddf6

# Cascadia Mono — the console font, and the reason 1.4.0 exists.
#
# **Why there is a font in this payload at all**, which 1.3.0 established and
# nothing here takes back. Measured on the device: `prefix/drive_c/windows/Fonts/`
# was empty, zero files, `HKCU\Console` had no `FaceName` value at all, and Claude
# Code's TUI drew every horizontal rule as `□□□□□`. That was missing glyphs and not
# a VT problem — colours and text rendered correctly and `patches/wine/0052`
# already parses the escape sequences. With `FaceName` empty conhost took
# `init_window`'s `!config.face_name[0]` branch and enumerated a face out of what
# there was: the Wine component's `.fon` bitmaps (coure.fon, cvgasys.fon,
# couree.fon …), its non-monospace TTFs (tahoma, symbol, marlett, webdings,
# wingding), and the `CutiveMono.ttf` / `DroidSansMono.ttf` win32u's
# `freetype_load_fonts` picks up from Android's `/system/fonts`. None of those has
# a U+2500 block, so a box was the correct thing for the renderer to draw.
#
# **GNU Unifont was tried first, dropped in 1.4.0, and is back in 1.5.0 as what
# this face falls back to rather than as a replacement for it.** 1.3.0 shipped
# Unifont 17.0.05 because it is the one font whose whole purpose is Unicode
# coverage, and it delivered exactly that: the box-drawing glyphs rendered on the
# device. 1.4.0 dropped it because it is bitmap-derived — 16x16 pixel grids
# converted to CFF outlines — and looks poor at any size; measured on the device, it
# works and it is ugly. Both releases were choosing between legible and complete,
# and that was never the only choice available: Windows resolves it per glyph
# through GDI font linking, Wine implements font linking, and 1.5.0 uses it. See
# TOOLS_UNIFONT_VERSION below for the mechanism and for what it does and does not
# buy. Cascadia stays the face; what follows about Cascadia is unchanged.
#
# **What Cascadia does and does not cover, which is now the first tier of a chain
# rather than the whole answer.** Counted out of the fonts' own cmaps on
# 2026-08-17 (format 12 subtable, the one with the most coverage):
#
#   Cascadia Mono   1,863 BMP +    563 above-BMP =   2,426 codepoints
#   Unifont        57,070 BMP +  1,840 above-BMP =  58,910
#   Unifont Upper        1 BMP + 59,055 above-BMP =  59,056
#
# Probed codepoint by codepoint rather than inferred from those ratios, Cascadia
# Mono has box drawing (U+2500, U+2502, U+250C, U+2550, U+256C), block elements and
# shades (U+2580, U+2588, U+2591), braille (U+2801), arrows (U+2190), Greek,
# Cyrillic, Hebrew (U+05D0) and Arabic (U+0627) — 100% of Box Drawing, Block
# Elements, Geometric Shapes and Braille. What it does not have: CJK (U+4E00),
# hiragana (U+3042), katakana (U+30A2), hangul (U+AC00), fullwidth forms (U+FF21),
# Devanagari (U+0915), Thai (U+0E01), and **12 glyphs Claude Code's own TUI draws**
# — `U+23FA` and `U+23BF`, which are on every tool-call line, the five spinner
# glyphs `U+273B U+273D U+2733 U+2734 U+2736`, the marks `U+2714 U+2717 U+2718`,
# `U+26A0` and `U+21B5`. Those 12 are why 1.5.0 exists; the linked chain below
# closes all 12, measured.
#
# **The powerline claim that arrived with this change is false, and is corrected
# here rather than repeated.** U+E0B0 and U+E0B2 are MISSING from
# `CascadiaMono-Regular` — probed, not assumed. Microsoft ships those in the
# separate `CascadiaMonoPL` and `CascadiaMonoNF` cuts inside the same archive.
# Unifont did not have them either, also probed. So nothing is lost: a powerline
# prompt has never rendered in this console and still will not.
#
# **Mono and not Code, static and not variable.** `Cascadia Code` is the same
# design with programming ligatures, which draw across cell boundaries in a fixed
# console grid — wrong for a terminal by construction, which is why Microsoft
# publishes the Mono cut at all. Static rather than variable because conhost asks
# GDI for a single weight through `CreateFontIndirectW`, and a variable font's
# default instance is a thing to avoid rather than to rely on.
#
# **TrueType, so the sfnt tag build/tools.sh asserts is `0x00010000` and not
# `OTTO`.** This is a `glyf`-outline font where Unifont was CFF. Wine does not care
# either way — `load_directory_fonts` hands every file in `windows\Fonts` to
# FreeType with no extension filter — but the tag is what catches a truncated
# download or an HTML error page arriving under a `.ttf` name.
#
# **One zip for every variant, and one member out of it.** Microsoft publishes a
# single archive holding static and variable builds of Cascadia Code, Cascadia Mono
# and their NF and PL cuts in TTF, OTF and WOFF2. build/tools.sh extracts exactly
# `ttf/static/CascadiaMono-Regular.ttf`, 575,912 bytes, and nothing else; the
# archive is 150 MB and the payload needs one face out of it.
#
# **The sha256 below is COMPUTED, not published.** GitHub release assets carry no
# checksum list, so like TOOLS_GET_PIP_SHA256 — and unlike Git's, Node's, Python's,
# PowerShell's and Temurin's — this is a record of what downloaded on 2026-08-17
# and not an upstream pin. A mismatch means the asset moved *or* the download is
# corrupt, and there is nothing upstream to arbitrate between them. Verified
# 2026-08-17, HTTP 200: CascadiaCode-2407.24.zip, 150,454,761 bytes, the newest
# release, published 2024-11-27.
#
# **Licence: OFL, with one wrinkle worth writing down.** microsoft/cascadia-code is
# OFL 1.1 and the font's own `name` ID 14 points at https://scripts.sil.org/OFL.
# `name` ID 13 nevertheless carries Microsoft's generic "Microsoft supplied font …
# as permitted by the license terms … of the Microsoft product" boilerplate, and
# the release zip ships no LICENSE member at all (checked by listing it). The
# repository licence is what governs; the boilerplate is noted so nobody reads it
# out of the font later and thinks the question was skipped.
#
# **The family name is `Cascadia Mono`, read out of the TTF rather than assumed.**
# build/tools.sh parses the `name` table — platform 3, encoding 1, name ID 1 — and
# asserts it equals TOOLS_CASCADIA_FAMILY, because PrefixRegistry.consoleColours
# seeds `FaceName` as a string and `CreateFontIndirectW` returns *some* font
# whatever happens: a wrong name gives a nearest match with no error and no log
# line, so the failure reads as a font that installed and did nothing.
#
# **1.5.0 gives that string a second reader, so it is now load-bearing twice.** It
# is also the *value name* under the SystemLink key — `load_system_links` keys the
# whole link list on the base font's family name (win32u/font.c:2050-2079) and
# `create_child_font_list` looks it up by `otm.otmpFaceName` (font.c:2775-2782). A
# name that does not match the installed family therefore fails in two places at
# once and silently in both.
#
# **One standing risk this retires, read off the tables: `post.isFixedPitch` is 1
# here where Unifont's was 0.** Unifont's pin carried the worry that win32u would
# build a variable-pitch TEXTMETRIC from it (freetype.c:3322, note the inverted
# meaning of TMPF_FIXED_PITCH); that is gone. Note PANOSE is all zeros in this
# font, so bProportion is 0 (Any) rather than 9 (Monospaced) — nothing on the
# `FaceName` path consults it, which is why it is a note and not a problem.
#
# **Unverified, and it is what reading cannot settle.** Nothing here has run on a
# device. That the glyphs appear and that the face is legible at conhost's default
# cell are the expectations behind this change, not results of it. See
# build/tools.sh's Fonts block and PrefixRegistry.consoleColours for the rest.
TOOLS_CASCADIA_VERSION=2407.24
TOOLS_CASCADIA_SHA256=e67a68ee3386db63f48b9054bd196ea752bc6a4ebb4df35adce6733da50c8474
# The family name the seed writes into HKCU\Console\FaceName. Pinned here so the
# build asserts what PrefixRegistry claims rather than the two drifting apart.
#
# **Quoted, and it is the first value in this file that has to be.** Every script
# sources this, so an unquoted space makes the shell run the second word as a
# command: `Mono: command not found`, exit 127, before a single build step ran.
# Unifont's family was one word and never exposed it — TOOLS_UNIFONT_UPPER_FAMILY
# below has the same shape and is quoted for the same reason.
TOOLS_CASCADIA_FAMILY="Cascadia Mono"

# GNU Unifont — the fallback face, and the reason 1.5.0 exists.
#
# **What the two previous releases got wrong, and it was the same thing both
# times.** 1.3.0 shipped Unifont for coverage and 1.4.0 replaced it with Cascadia
# for legibility, and each wrote down the other's property as the price. The
# sentence that made that look inevitable appears in 1.4.0's commit, in
# build/tools.sh, in PrefixRegistry and in app/build.gradle.kts: "conhost resolves
# one face with no font linking". **The second half of that is false.** conhost does
# resolve one face — it calls `CreateFontIndirectW` once and selects the result —
# but it never had to resolve more than one, because the fallback is not conhost's
# job. It is GDI's, it happens a layer below, and Wine implements it.
#
# **The mechanism, read in the tree we ship (native/wine/dlls/win32u/font.c) and
# not in Microsoft's documentation.** Four hops, each verified:
#
#   1. `system_link_keyW` (font.c:107-118) is
#      `\Registry\Machine\Software\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\FontLink\SystemLink`.
#      Wine reads the same key Windows does.
#   2. `load_system_links` (font.c:2030-2085) enumerates that key's values. **The
#      value NAME is the base font family** and the data is a run of
#      NUL-terminated `filename[,familyname]` strings, walked until a zero-length
#      entry or the data runs out. Each is resolved by `find_face_from_filename`
#      (font.c:896-911), which compares the **basename** of the registered font's
#      path, case-insensitively (`family_find_face_from_filename`, font.c:879-893).
#   3. `create_child_font_list` (font.c:2775-2816) attaches the resolved families
#      as child fonts, called unconditionally from `create_gdi_font` (font.c:4533)
#      for every font anything selects.
#   4. `get_glyph_index_linked` (font.c:3757-3775) is the payoff: try the base
#      font, and on a miss walk the children **in list order** and switch to the
#      first that has the glyph. Called from `get_glyph_outline` (font.c:3799).
#
# **And it reaches conhost, which is the hop worth spelling out because a
# mechanism that only applies to `GetGlyphOutline` calls nobody makes is no use.**
# conhost draws into `CreateCompatibleDC(0)` (programs/conhost/window.c:2083) — a
# memory DC, so the DIB engine, not the X11 driver. The DIB engine rasterises text
# through `cache_glyph_bitmap` (win32u/dibdrv/graphics.c:745-780), which calls
# `NtGdiGetGlyphOutline` per glyph; that reaches `font_GetGlyphOutline`
# (font.c:4082) and therefore `get_glyph_outline` and the linked lookup above. So
# the fallback applies to exactly the text this whole change is about.
#
# **The chain, and the order is the design rather than an accident.**
# `get_glyph_index_linked` takes the FIRST child with the glyph, so the list runs
# best-typography-first and guaranteed-coverage-last:
#
#   Cascadia Mono                          the face itself; everything a TUI draws
#   NotoSansSymbols-Regular-Subsetted.ttf  the spinners and marks; symlinked, 0 bytes shipped
#   unifont-17.0.05.otf                    U+23FA, U+23BF, and the whole BMP
#   unifont_upper-17.0.05.otf              planes 1-15
#
# Two files are in the payload and one is symlinked off the device — see
# `SessionRuntime.linkAndroidFonts`. Measured on the device: `/system/fonts` holds
# 211 fonts, Android's Noto set, and `NotoSansSymbols-Regular-Subsetted.ttf`
# (708,968 bytes, 4,616 codepoints) covers all five spinner glyphs, all five status
# marks, and 100% of Box Drawing, Block Elements and Braille — but **not** U+23FA or
# U+23BF, which is why Unifont is still required and not merely nice. Linking rather
# than copying because the file is already on the device; the payload cost is zero.
#
# **What the union covers, measured rather than argued.** Union of Cascadia,
# Unifont and Unifont Upper: 57,070 BMP + 59,055 above-BMP = 116,125 codepoints,
# and **all 12 of the glyphs Cascadia was missing**, 100% of Box Drawing, Block
# Elements, Geometric Shapes, Braille, CJK Unified, Hiragana, Hangul Syllables and
# the emoji blocks. Cascadia's 1,863 BMP codepoints are a strict subset of
# Unifont's 57,070, which is what makes the two-tier chain complete rather than
# merely larger.
#
# **Five tiers were measured and four were rejected, so the omissions are choices.**
# `NotoSansCJK-Regular.ttc` (32,355,424 bytes), `NotoNaskhArabic-Regular.ttf`,
# `NotoSansHebrew-Regular.ttf`, `NotoSansDevanagari-VF.ttf`,
# `NotoSansThai-Regular.ttf` and `NotoColorEmoji.ttf` are all on the device and none
# is linked:
#
#   - CJK would be a better typeface for something conhost cannot lay out at all.
#     `programs/conhost/window.c:725` and `:797` both carry
#     "FIXME: use maximum width for DBCS codepages since some chars take two
#     cells", and the draw loop pins every glyph to `i * font.width` with
#     `dx[] = font.width` (window.c:483). A full-width glyph overlaps its
#     neighbour whatever drew it. Unifont still covers CJK, so nothing is tofu.
#   - Arabic, Hebrew, Devanagari and Thai need shaping and conhost does none — no
#     Uniscribe, no HarfBuzz, one `ExtTextOutW` glyph per cell. Arabic comes out as
#     isolated unjoined forms however good the font is.
#   - `NotoColorEmoji.ttf` is CBDT/CBLC colour bitmaps and Wine's FreeType path
#     rasterises outlines. An entry that probably does nothing is worse than none,
#     because it makes the chain look like it handles colour emoji. **Unifont
#     Upper is what actually backs emoji, and it is monochrome outlines.**
#
# Microsoft's own fonts were the other obvious answer and are rejected outright:
# Consolas, Segoe UI, MS Gothic and Segoe UI Emoji are proprietary and cannot be
# redistributed in a public release. The chain is built from what is either OFL or
# already on the device for exactly that reason.
#
# **There is no case for subsetting Unifont, and the 1.3.0 measurement is why.**
# Its two files are 11,460,416 raw bytes and cost **315,196 packed** in the .wcp.
# A subset would save almost nothing and would destroy the one property that makes
# Unifont worth shipping, which is that arbitrary text never comes out empty.
#
# **OTF and not TTF: 17.0.05 publishes no TTF build.** unifoundry's
# `font-builds/*.ttf` paths 404 for this release. Wine does not care —
# `load_directory_fonts` (font.c) hands every file in `windows\Fonts` to FreeType
# with no extension filter at all, and FreeType reads CFF. The sfnt tag
# build/tools.sh asserts for these two is therefore `OTTO`, where Cascadia's is
# `0x00010000`.
#
# **The hashes below are COMPUTED, not published.** GNU ships a detached `.sig` GPG
# signature per file rather than a checksum list, so unlike Git's, Node's,
# Python's, PowerShell's and Temurin's, these are a record of what downloaded on
# 2026-08-17 and not an upstream pin. A mismatch means the file moved *or* the
# download is corrupt and there is nothing upstream to arbitrate between them —
# same standing as TOOLS_GET_PIP_SHA256, and treated the same way. Verified
# 2026-08-17, HTTP 200 from ftp.gnu.org:
#
#   unifont-17.0.05.otf        5,321,628 bytes
#   unifont_upper-17.0.05.otf  6,138,788 bytes
#
# **`unifont_upper` is a SECOND FAMILY, not a style of the first**, and that is
# asserted rather than assumed: `Unifont Upper` is what its `name` table says. It
# matters because the SystemLink chain lists both files and
# `find_face_from_filename` resolves each to a family; two files claiming one
# family would let GDI resolve `FaceName` to the plane-1 file, which has 1 BMP
# codepoint in it.
#
# **Unverified, and it is what reading cannot settle.** Nothing here has run on a
# device. Reading says the `.reg` import writes a REG_MULTI_SZ that
# `load_system_links` will parse and that the chain resolves; whether the 12 glyphs
# appear, and whether a 16x16-bitmap-derived glyph sitting next to Cascadia
# outlines looks acceptable rather than merely present, cannot be settled by
# reading. The known-wrong-on-purpose part is geometry: see the DBCS FIXME above.
TOOLS_UNIFONT_VERSION=17.0.05
TOOLS_UNIFONT_SHA256=85701ab9b1e251ee16f4df00b13f22eac311d72b7dab427a7d975fe7f5064702
TOOLS_UNIFONT_UPPER_SHA256=f4fd6d5d752726d384feef175bb780c9f29382cd4941c9e1e6990d7c3822a090
# The two family names, read out of the OTFs' `name` tables and asserted by
# build/tools.sh. Neither is written to the registry as a face name — the seed
# names files, not families, because `find_face_from_filename` matches on the
# filename — but a family name that changes upstream is a signal worth breaking
# the build over, and the "these are two families" assertion needs both.
TOOLS_UNIFONT_FAMILY=Unifont
# Quoted for TOOLS_CASCADIA_FAMILY's reason: every script sources this file, so an
# unquoted space runs `Upper` as a command and the build dies at exit 127 before it
# does anything.
TOOLS_UNIFONT_UPPER_FAMILY="Unifont Upper"

# **The exact filenames PrefixRegistry writes into the SystemLink value.**
#
# Spelled literally here rather than composed from TOOLS_UNIFONT_VERSION, because
# the coupling this guards is between a shell script and a Kotlin constant and only
# a literal can be compared across that gap. `find_face_from_filename` matches on
# the basename (win32u/font.c:879-893), and a name that does not resolve is dropped
# with a `TRACE` line and nothing else (font.c:2076) — so bumping
# TOOLS_UNIFONT_VERSION without editing `PrefixRegistry.FONT_LINK_CHAIN` would
# stage `unifont-17.0.06.otf`, seed a link to `unifont-17.0.05.otf`, and silently
# lose the fallback with the build green and the payload correct.
#
# build/tools.sh asserts that these are the names it staged; PrefixRegistryTest
# asserts the seed spells the same three. Change one and both fail.
TOOLS_FONTLINK_UNIFONT_FILE=unifont-17.0.05.otf
TOOLS_FONTLINK_UNIFONT_UPPER_FILE=unifont_upper-17.0.05.otf
# The device's own file, symlinked rather than shipped, so the build cannot assert
# this one — it is here so the chain is readable in one place. Measured present on
# the device 2026-08-17; `/system/fonts` names vary by Android version and OEM, and
# a missing file degrades to the next tier by construction.
TOOLS_FONTLINK_NOTO_SYMBOLS_FILE=NotoSansSymbols-Regular-Subsetted.ttf

# --- Toolchain ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Pinned in the Dockerfile, restated here so one file answers "what versions?".
#
# NDK r29 is REQUIRED, and the reason is not the clang version number.
#
# Verified by probe on 2026-08-07:
#   r28c -> clang "19.0.1" (Android build of LLVM snapshot r530567e)
#           --print-supported-cpus tops out at cortex-x4. NO oryon-1.
#           Both -mcpu=oryon-1 and -mtune=oryon-1 are REJECTED.
#   r29  -> clang "21.0.0" (snapshot r563880c). oryon-1 present, both
#           -mcpu=oryon-1 and -mtune=oryon-1 accepted.
#
# oryon-1 landed in upstream LLVM 19, but Android's clang 19 is built from a
# pre-oryon snapshot, so the advertised version number is not a reliable proxy.
# Box64's SD8EG5 preset hardcodes -mtune=oryon-1, so on r28c the build fails at
# compile time. Do not "downgrade to a known-good NDK" without re-probing.
ANDROID_NDK_VERSION=r29
ANDROID_API_LEVEL=36

# llvm-mingw provides the arm64ec-w64-mingw32 target used for FEX's PE DLLs
# and Wine's ARM64EC build.
LLVM_MINGW_VERSION=20250910

# --- Target ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Selects the flag set in build/targets/<TARGET>.env
TARGET=canoe
